return [
    // Title/breadcrumb extras
    'Pause-based desync attacks' => 'Serangan pause-based desync',
    'Browser requirements' => 'Persyaratan browser',
    'Note on HTTP/2 message representation' => 'Catatan tentang representasi pesan HTTP/2',
    'SecDevLab Research' => 'Riset SecDevLab',
    'What is the difference between web cache poisoning and web cache deception?' => 'Apa perbedaan antara web cache poisoning dan web cache deception?',

    // request-smuggling overview
    'HTTP request smuggling' => 'HTTP request smuggling',
    "In this section, we'll explain HTTP request smuggling attacks and describe how common request smuggling vulnerabilities can arise." => 'Pada bagian ini, kami menjelaskan serangan HTTP request smuggling dan bagaimana kerentanan request smuggling umum muncul.',
    "If you're already familiar with HTTP request smuggling and just want to practice on a series of deliberately vulnerable sites, check out the link below for an overview of all labs in this topic." => 'Jika Anda sudah memahami HTTP request smuggling dan ingin langsung berlatih di situs target yang sengaja dibuat rentan, lihat daftar lengkap lab topik ini di bawah.',
    'View all HTTP request smuggling labs' => 'Lihat semua lab HTTP request smuggling',
    'What is HTTP request smuggling?' => 'Apa itu HTTP request smuggling?',
    'HTTP request smuggling is a technique for interfering with the way a web site processes sequences of HTTP requests that are received from one or more users. Request smuggling vulnerabilities are often critical in nature, allowing an attacker to bypass security controls, gain unauthorized access to sensitive data, and directly compromise other application users.' => 'HTTP request smuggling adalah teknik memanipulasi cara situs web memproses rangkaian permintaan HTTP dari satu atau beberapa pengguna. Kerentanan ini sering berstatus kritis karena memungkinkan penyerang melewati kontrol keamanan, mengakses data sensitif secara tidak sah, dan mengkompromikan pengguna aplikasi lain.',
    'Request smuggling is primarily associated with HTTP/1 requests. However, websites that support HTTP/2 may be vulnerable, depending on their back-end architecture.' => 'Request smuggling terutama berkaitan dengan HTTP/1. Namun situs yang mendukung HTTP/2 tetap bisa rentan, bergantung pada arsitektur back-end yang dipakai.',
    "HTTP request smuggling was first documented in 2005, and repopularized by SecDevLab's extensive research on the topic. For details, check out the following whitepapers:" => 'HTTP request smuggling pertama kali didokumentasikan pada 2005 dan kembali populer lewat riset mendalam tentang desinkronisasi HTTP. Untuk detailnya, simak makalah berikut:',
    'HTTP desync attacks: Request smuggling reborn' => 'Serangan HTTP desync: kembalinya request smuggling',
    'HTTP/2: The sequel is always worse' => 'HTTP/2: sekuel yang lebih berisiko',
    'Browser-powered desync attacks: A new frontier in HTTP request smuggling' => 'Serangan desync berbasis browser: babak baru HTTP request smuggling',
    'What happens in an HTTP request smuggling attack?' => 'Apa yang terjadi dalam serangan HTTP request smuggling?',
    "Today's web applications frequently employ chains of HTTP servers between users and the ultimate application logic. Users send requests to a front-end server (sometimes called a load balancer or reverse proxy) and this server forwards requests to one or more back-end servers. This type of architecture is increasingly common, and in some cases unavoidable, in modern cloud-based applications." => 'Aplikasi web modern kerap menempatkan rantai server HTTP di antara pengguna dan logika aplikasi inti: server front-end (load balancer atau reverse proxy) meneruskan permintaan ke server back-end.',
    'When the front-end server forwards HTTP requests to a back-end server, it typically sends several requests over the same back-end network connection, because this is much more efficient and performant. The protocol is very simple; HTTP requests are sent one after another, and the receiving server has to determine where one request ends and the next one begins:' => 'Saat meneruskan permintaan HTTP ke back-end, front-end umumnya mengirim beberapa permintaan melalui koneksi jaringan yang sama demi efisiensi. Permintaan dikirim berurutan dan server penerima harus menentukan di mana satu permintaan berakhir dan permintaan berikutnya dimulai:',
    'In this situation, it is crucial that the front-end and back-end systems agree about the boundaries between requests. Otherwise, an attacker might be able to send an ambiguous request that gets interpreted differently by the front-end and back-end systems:' => 'Dalam arsitektur ini, front-end dan back-end wajib sepakat soal batas tiap permintaan. Jika tidak, penyerang dapat mengirim permintaan ambigu yang ditafsirkan berbeda oleh keduanya:',
    'Here, the attacker causes part of their front-end request to be interpreted by the back-end server as the start of the next request. It is effectively prepended to the next request, and so can interfere with the way the application processes that request. This is a request smuggling attack, and it can have devastating results.' => 'Di sini, sebagian permintaan penyerang dibaca back-end sebagai awal permintaan berikutnya dan disisipkan di depan permintaan pengguna lain, sehingga mengganggu cara aplikasi memprosesnya.',
    'How do HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities arise?' => 'Bagaimana kerentanan HTTP request smuggling muncul?',
    'Most HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities arise because the HTTP/1 specification provides two different ways to specify where a request ends: the [[C0]] header and the [[C1]] header.' => 'Sebagian besar celah HTTP request smuggling muncul karena spesifikasi HTTP/1 menyediakan dua cara berbeda untuk menentukan panjang pesan: header [[C0]] dan [[C1]].',
    'The [[C0]] header is straightforward: it specifies the length of the message body in bytes. For example:' => 'Header [[C0]] bekerja sederhana dengan menetapkan panjang body pesan dalam satuan byte. Contohnya:',
    'The [[C0]] header can be used to specify that the message body uses chunked encoding. This means that the message body contains one or more chunks of data. Each chunk consists of the chunk size in bytes (expressed in hexadecimal), followed by a newline, followed by the chunk contents. The message is terminated with a chunk of size zero. For example:' => 'Header [[C0]] menandakan body memakai chunked encoding: beberapa blok data yang diawali ukuran blok dalam heksadesimal, baris baru, isi blok, lalu ditutup blok berukuran nol. Contohnya:',
    'Many security testers are unaware that chunked encoding can be used in HTTP requests, for two reasons:' => 'Banyak penguji keamanan tidak menyadari bahwa chunked encoding juga bisa dipakai di permintaan HTTP karena dua hal:',
    'Burp Suite automatically unpacks chunked encoding to make messages easier to view and edit.' => 'Alat proksi HTTP umumnya membongkar chunked encoding secara otomatis agar pesan lebih mudah dibaca dan disunting.',
    'Browsers do not normally use chunked encoding in requests, and it is normally seen only in server responses.' => 'Browser jarang memakai chunked encoding pada permintaan; format ini lebih lazim muncul di respons server.',
    'As the HTTP/1 specification provides two different methods for specifying the length of HTTP messages, it is possible for a single message to use both methods at once, such that they conflict with each other. The specification attempts to prevent this problem by stating that if both the [[C0]] and [[C1]] headers are present, then the [[C2]] header should be ignored. This might be sufficient to avoid ambiguity when only a single server is in play, but not when two or more servers are chained together. In this situation, problems can arise for two reasons:' => 'Karena ada dua cara menentukan panjang pesan, keduanya bisa dipasang bersamaan dan saling bertentangan. Spesifikasi mewajibkan [[C2]] diabaikan bila [[C0]] dan [[C1]] sama-sama hadir, namun aturan ini sering dilanggar saat dua server berantai karena dua alasan:',
    'Some servers do not support the [[C0]] header in requests.' => 'Sebagian server tidak mendukung header [[C0]] pada permintaan.',
    'Some servers that do support the [[C0]] header can be induced not to process it if the header is obfuscated in some way.' => 'Sebagian server yang mendukung [[C0]] bisa dibuat mengabaikannya bila header tersebut disamarkan.',
    'If the front-end and back-end servers behave differently in relation to the (possibly obfuscated) [[C0]] header, then they might disagree about the boundaries between successive requests, leading to request smuggling vulnerabilities.' => 'Jika front-end dan back-end memperlakukan header [[C0]] secara berbeda, keduanya akan berselisih soal batas antar-permintaan dan memicu request smuggling.',
    'Websites that use HTTP/2 end-to-end are inherently immune to request smuggling attacks. As the HTTP/2 specification introduces a single, robust mechanism for specifying the length of a request, there is no way for an attacker to introduce the required ambiguity.' => 'Situs yang memakai HTTP/2 secara end-to-end pada dasarnya kebal terhadap request smuggling karena hanya mengenal satu mekanisme panjang pesan berbasis frame biner.',
    'However, many websites have an HTTP/2-speaking front-end server, but deploy this in front of back-end infrastructure that only supports HTTP/1. This means that the front-end effectively has to translate the requests it receives into HTTP/1. This process is known as HTTP downgrading. For more information, see [[A0]]Advanced request smuggling[[/A0]].' => 'Namun banyak situs memakai front-end HTTP/2 di depan back-end HTTP/1, sehingga front-end menerjemahkan permintaan ke HTTP/1 melalui proses HTTP downgrading. Lihat detailnya di [[A0]]Request smuggling lanjutan[[/A0]].',
    'How to perform an HTTP request smuggling attack' => 'Cara melancarkan serangan HTTP request smuggling',
    'Classic request smuggling attacks involve placing both the [[C0]] header and the [[C1]] header into a single HTTP/1 request and manipulating these so that the front-end and back-end servers process the request differently. The exact way in which this is done depends on the behavior of the two servers:' => 'Serangan request smuggling klasik menempatkan header [[C0]] dan [[C1]] sekaligus dalam satu permintaan HTTP/1 agar diproses berbeda oleh front-end dan back-end, bergantung perilaku tiap server:',
    'CL.TE: the front-end server uses the [[C0]] header and the back-end server uses the [[C1]] header.' => 'CL.TE: front-end memakai [[C0]], sedangkan back-end memakai [[C1]].',
    'TE.CL: the front-end server uses the [[C0]] header and the back-end server uses the [[C1]] header.' => 'TE.CL: front-end memakai [[C0]], sedangkan back-end memakai [[C1]].',
    'TE.TE: the front-end and back-end servers both support the [[C0]] header, but one of the servers can be induced not to process it by obfuscating the header in some way.' => 'TE.TE: keduanya mendukung [[C0]], namun salah satunya dapat diakali agar mengabaikan header tersebut lewat penyamaran.',
    'These techniques are only possible using HTTP/1 requests. Browsers and other clients, including Burp, use HTTP/2 by default to communicate with servers that explicitly advertise support for it during the TLS handshake.' => 'Teknik ini hanya bekerja melalui HTTP/1, padahal browser dan klien modern secara default memakai HTTP/2 saat bernegosiasi TLS.',
    'As a result, when testing sites with HTTP/2 support, you need to manually switch protocols in Burp Repeater. You can do this from the <strong>Request attributes</strong> section of the <strong>Inspector</strong> panel.' => 'Saat menguji situs berdukungan HTTP/2, ubah protokol secara manual ke HTTP/1 melalui panel atribut permintaan di alat penguji Anda.',
    'CL.TE vulnerabilities' => 'Kerentanan CL.TE',
    'Here, the front-end server uses the [[C0]] header and the back-end server uses the [[C1]] header. We can perform a simple HTTP request smuggling attack as follows:' => 'Di sini front-end memakai [[C0]] sedangkan back-end memakai [[C1]]. Berikut contoh serangan sederhana:',
    'The front-end server processes the [[C0]] header and determines that the request body is 13 bytes long, up to the end of [[C1]]. This request is forwarded on to the back-end server.' => 'Front-end membaca header [[C0]] dan menilai body berukuran 13 byte sampai akhir [[C1]], lalu meneruskannya utuh ke back-end.',
    'The back-end server processes the [[C0]] header, and so treats the message body as using chunked encoding. It processes the first chunk, which is stated to be zero length, and so is treated as terminating the request. The following bytes, [[C1]], are left unprocessed, and the back-end server will treat these as being the start of the next request in the sequence.' => 'Back-end justru membaca header [[C0]], menemui chunk nol byte sebagai akhir permintaan, lalu memperlakukan byte tersisa [[C1]] sebagai awal permintaan berikutnya.',
    'TE.CL vulnerabilities' => 'Kerentanan TE.CL',
    'Here, the front-end server uses the [[C0]] header and the back-end server uses the [[C1]] header. We can perform a simple HTTP request smuggling attack as follows:' => 'Di sini front-end memakai [[C0]] dan back-end memakai [[C1]]. Serangan dapat dikirim seperti berikut:',
    'To send this request using Burp Repeater, you will first need to go to the Repeater menu and ensure that the "Update Content-Length" option is unchecked.' => 'Saat mengirim permintaan ini, pastikan fitur pembaruan otomatis Content-Length pada alat penguji Anda dinonaktifkan.',
    'You need to include the trailing sequence [[C0]] following the final [[C1]].' => 'Sertakan rangkaian akhiran [[C0]] tepat setelah angka [[C1]] terakhir.',
    'The front-end server processes the [[C0]] header, and so treats the message body as using chunked encoding. It processes the first chunk, which is stated to be 8 bytes long, up to the start of the line following [[C1]]. It processes the second chunk, which is stated to be zero length, and so is treated as terminating the request. This request is forwarded on to the back-end server.' => 'Front-end memakai [[C0]], membaca chunk 8 byte sampai setelah [[C1]] lalu chunk penutup 0, dan meneruskan semuanya ke back-end.',
    'The back-end server processes the [[C0]] header and determines that the request body is 3 bytes long, up to the start of the line following [[C1]]. The following bytes, starting with [[C2]], are left unprocessed, and the back-end server will treat these as being the start of the next request in the sequence.' => 'Back-end memakai [[C0]] sehingga hanya membaca 3 byte sampai baris [[C1]]. Sisa byte mulai dari [[C2]] dibiarkan dan diperlakukan sebagai awal permintaan berikutnya.',
    'TE.TE behavior: obfuscating the TE header' => 'Perilaku TE.TE: menyamarkan header TE',
    'Here, the front-end and back-end servers both support the [[C0]] header, but one of the servers can be induced not to process it by obfuscating the header in some way.' => 'Baik front-end maupun back-end mendukung [[C0]], tetapi salah satunya bisa dibuat mengabaikannya dengan menyamarkan header tersebut.',
    'There are potentially endless ways to obfuscate the [[C0]] header. For example:' => 'Ada sangat banyak variasi penyamaran header [[C0]]. Contohnya:',
    'Each of these techniques involves a subtle departure from the HTTP specification. Real-world code that implements a protocol specification rarely adheres to it with absolute precision, and it is common for different implementations to tolerate different variations from the specification. To uncover a TE.TE vulnerability, it is necessary to find some variation of the [[C0]] header such that only one of the front-end or back-end servers processes it, while the other server ignores it.' => 'Tiap teknik menyimpang halus dari spesifikasi HTTP. Untuk menemukan celah TE.TE, cari variasi [[C0]] yang hanya dipahami oleh satu server dan diabaikan server lainnya.',
    'Depending on whether it is the front-end or the back-end server that can be induced not to process the obfuscated [[C0]] header, the remainder of the attack will take the same form as for the CL.TE or TE.CL vulnerabilities already described.' => 'Bergantung server mana yang mengabaikan [[C0]], kelanjutan serangan mengikuti pola CL.TE atau TE.CL.',
    'How to identify HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities' => 'Cara mengidentifikasi kerentanan HTTP request smuggling',
    "Check out the following section for some tips on how to identify HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities for yourself. We've also provided some interactive [[S0]]LABS[[/S0]], so you can see how this works in practice." => 'Simak bagian berikut untuk kiat menemukan kerentanan HTTP request smuggling beserta [[S0]]LAB PRAKTIK[[/S0]] interaktifnya.',
    'Finding HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities' => 'Menemukan kerentanan HTTP request smuggling',
    'How to exploit HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities' => 'Cara mengeksploitasi kerentanan HTTP request smuggling',
    "Now that you're familiar with the basic concepts, let's take a look at how HTTP request smuggling can be used to craft a number of high-severity attacks. As usual, there are plenty of fully interactive [[S0]]LABS[[/S0]], so you can try your hand at attacking realistic targets." => 'Setelah memahami konsep dasar, mari lihat bagaimana request smuggling dirangkai menjadi serangan berdampak tinggi pada target [[S0]]LAB PRAKTIK[[/S0]].',
    'Exploiting HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities' => 'Mengeksploitasi kerentanan HTTP request smuggling',
    'Advanced HTTP request smuggling' => 'HTTP request smuggling lanjutan',
    "If you've already completed the rest of our request smuggling labs, you're ready to learn some more advanced techniques. We've created a number of interactive [[S0]]LABS[[/S0]] based on real-world vulnerabilities discovered by SecDevLab researchers. You'll even get a chance to try out Burp's one-of-a-kind features for HTTP/2-based testing." => 'Setelah menyelesaikan lab dasar, lanjutkan ke teknik lanjutan beserta [[S0]]LAB PRAKTIK[[/S0]] yang terinspirasi celah nyata termasuk vektor berbasis HTTP/2.',
    'Advanced HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities' => 'Kerentanan HTTP request smuggling lanjutan',
    'Browser-powered request smuggling' => 'Request smuggling berbasis browser',
    "The request smuggling techniques you've learned so far rely on sending intentionally malformed requests using dedicated hacking tools like Burp Repeater. In fact, it's possible to perform the same attacks using fully browser-compatible requests that desync the two servers using a perfectly normal [[C0]] header." => 'Teknik sejauh ini mengandalkan permintaan cacat buatan alat khusus. Nyatanya, desinkronisasi juga dapat dipicu memakai permintaan HTTP sah dari browser dengan header [[C0]] biasa.',
    "This even enables you to launch client-side variations of these attacks, which induce a victim's browser to poison its own connection to the vulnerable website. Not only does this expose single-server sites to request smuggling-style attacks, it even enables you to attack sites that you don't have access to directly. Check out the learning materials and [[S0]]LABS[[/S0]] to learn how." => 'Hal ini membuka variasi sisi klien saat browser korban meracuni koneksinya sendiri, bahkan pada situs server tunggal maupun intranet. Pelajari materi dan [[S0]]LAB PRAKTIK[[/S0]] terkait.',
    'How to prevent HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities' => 'Cara mencegah kerentanan HTTP request smuggling',
    'HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities arise in situations where the front-end server and back-end server use different mechanisms for determining the boundaries between requests. This may be due to discrepancies between whether HTTP/1 servers use the [[C0]] header or chunked transfer encoding to determine where each request ends. In HTTP/2 environments, the common practice of [[A0]]downgrading HTTP/2 requests[[/A0]] for the back-end is also fraught with issues and enables or simplifies a number of additional attacks.' => 'Celah ini muncul akibat front-end dan back-end memakai aturan batas permintaan berbeda. Pada lingkungan HTTP/2, kebiasaan [[A0]]downgrading HTTP/2[[/A0]] ke back-end juga membuka banyak vektor serangan baru.',
    'To prevent HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities, we recommend the following high-level measures:' => 'Untuk mencegah HTTP request smuggling, terapkan langkah-langkah berikut:',
    'Use HTTP/2 end to end and disable HTTP downgrading if possible. HTTP/2 uses a robust mechanism for determining the length of requests and, when used end to end, is inherently protected against request smuggling. If you can\'t avoid HTTP downgrading, make sure you validate the rewritten request against the HTTP/1.1 specification. For example, reject requests that contain newlines in the headers, colons in header names, and spaces in the request method.' => 'Gunakan HTTP/2 end-to-end dan nonaktifkan HTTP downgrading bila memungkinkan. Jika downgrading tetap perlu, validasi ketat permintaan hasil konversi agar menolak newline di header, titik dua di nama header, maupun spasi di metode.',
    'Make the front-end server normalize ambiguous requests and make the back-end server reject any that are still ambiguous, closing the TCP connection in the process.' => 'Pastikan front-end menormalisasi permintaan ambigu dan back-end menolak keras permintaan ambigu dengan langsung menutup koneksi TCP.',
    'Never assume that requests won\'t have a body. This is the fundamental cause of both CL.0 and client-side desync vulnerabilities.' => 'Jangan pernah berasumsi permintaan tertentu pasti tanpa body, karena asumsi inilah akar dari celah CL.0 dan client-side desync.',
    'Default to discarding the connection if server-level exceptions are triggered when handling requests.' => 'Putuskan koneksi secara default apabila terjadi exception tingkat server saat memproses permintaan.',
    'If you route traffic through a forward proxy, ensure that upstream HTTP/2 is enabled if possible.' => 'Jika meneruskan trafik lewat forward proxy, aktifkan HTTP/2 ke upstream bila didukung.',
    "As we've demonstrated in the learning materials, disabling reuse of back-end connections will help to mitigate certain kinds of attack, but this still doesn't protect you from [[A0]]request tunnelling[[/A0]] attacks." => 'Menonaktifkan pemakaian ulang koneksi back-end memitigasi sebagian serangan, namun belum melindungi dari serangan [[A0]]request tunnelling[[/A0]].',
    'Find HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities using Burp Suite\'s web vulnerability scanner' => 'Temukan celah HTTP request smuggling dengan pemindai otomatis',

    // finding
    "In this section, we'll explain different techniques for finding HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities." => 'Pada bagian ini, kami menjelaskan berbagai teknik untuk mendeteksi kerentanan HTTP request smuggling.',
    'Finding HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities using timing techniques' => 'Menemukan request smuggling dengan teknik penundaan waktu',
    "The most generally effective way to detect HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities is to send requests that will cause a time delay in the application's responses if a vulnerability is present. This technique is used by Burp Scanner to automate the detection of request smuggling vulnerabilities." => 'Cara paling efektif mendeteksi request smuggling adalah mengirim permintaan yang memicu jeda waktu (time delay) di respons aplikasi apabila rentan.',
    'Finding CL.TE vulnerabilities using timing techniques' => 'Mendeteksi kerentanan CL.TE dengan teknik waktu',
    'If an application is vulnerable to the CL.TE variant of request smuggling, then sending a request like the following will often cause a time delay:' => 'Jika aplikasi rentan terhadap CL.TE, mengirim permintaan seperti berikut kerap menimbulkan jeda respons yang terukur:',
    'Since the front-end server uses the [[C0]] header, it will forward only part of this request, omitting the [[C1]]. The back-end server uses the [[C2]] header, processes the first chunk, and then waits for the next chunk to arrive. This will cause an observable time delay.' => 'Karena front-end memakai [[C0]], hanya sebagian permintaan yang diteruskan tanpa [[C1]]. Back-end memakai [[C2]] lalu menunggu chunk berikutnya sehingga memicu jeda waktu.',
    'Finding TE.CL vulnerabilities using timing techniques' => 'Mendeteksi kerentanan TE.CL dengan teknik waktu',
    'If an application is vulnerable to the TE.CL variant of request smuggling, then sending a request like the following will often cause a time delay:' => 'Jika aplikasi rentan terhadap TE.CL, permintaan seperti ini sering menyebabkan jeda waktu:',
    'Since the front-end server uses the [[C0]] header, it will forward only part of this request, omitting the [[C1]]. The back-end server uses the [[C2]] header, expects more content in the message body, and waits for the remaining content to arrive. This will cause an observable time delay.' => 'Front-end memakai [[C0]] sehingga meneruskan tanpa [[C1]], sedangkan back-end memakai [[C2]] dan menunggu sisa body hingga terjadi jeda waktu.',
    'The timing-based test for TE.CL vulnerabilities will potentially disrupt other application users if the application is vulnerable to the CL.TE variant of the vulnerability. So to be stealthy and minimize disruption, you should use the CL.TE test first and continue to the TE.CL test only if the first test is unsuccessful.' => 'Uji waktu TE.CL berpotensi mengganggu pengguna lain bila target ternyata rentan CL.TE. Jalankan uji CL.TE terlebih dahulu dan lanjut ke TE.CL hanya jika uji pertama tidak memicu jeda.',
    'Confirming HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities using differential responses' => 'Mengonfirmasi request smuggling lewat perbedaan respons',
    "When a probable request smuggling vulnerability has been detected, you can obtain further evidence for the vulnerability by exploiting it to trigger differences in the contents of the application's responses. This involves sending two requests to the application in quick succession:" => 'Setelah terindikasi rentan, perkuat bukti dengan memicu perbedaan isi respons memakai dua permintaan berurutan secara cepat:',
    'An "attack" request that is designed to interfere with the processing of the next request.' => 'Permintaan "serangan" untuk mengganggu pemrosesan permintaan sesudahnya.',
    'A "normal" request.' => 'Permintaan "normal".',
    'If the response to the normal request contains the expected interference, then the vulnerability is confirmed.' => 'Jika respons permintaan normal berubah sesuai skenario gangguan, kerentanan terbukti.',
    'For example, suppose the normal request looks like this:' => 'Misalnya, permintaan normal terlihat seperti berikut:',
    'This request normally receives an HTTP response with status code 200, containing some search results.' => 'Normalnya permintaan ini mendapat kode status 200 dengan hasil pencarian.',
    'The attack request that is needed to interfere with this request depends on the variant of request smuggling that is present: CL.TE vs TE.CL.' => 'Bentuk permintaan serangannya bergantung pada varian celah yang ada: CL.TE atau TE.CL.',
    'Confirming CL.TE vulnerabilities using differential responses' => 'Mengonfirmasi kerentanan CL.TE lewat perbedaan respons',
    'To confirm a CL.TE vulnerability, you would send an attack request like this:' => 'Untuk mengonfirmasi CL.TE, kirim permintaan serangan seperti berikut:',
    'If the attack is successful, then the last two lines of this request are treated by the back-end server as belonging to the next request that is received. This will cause the subsequent "normal" request to look like this:' => 'Jika berhasil, dua baris terakhir dianggap bagian permintaan berikutnya oleh back-end sehingga permintaan normal sesudahnya berubah menjadi:',
    'Since this request now contains an invalid URL, the server will respond with status code 404, indicating that the attack request did indeed interfere with it.' => 'Karena URL menjadi tidak valid, server membalas status 404, membuktikan adanya gangguan antar-permintaan.',
    'Confirming TE.CL vulnerabilities using differential responses' => 'Mengonfirmasi kerentanan TE.CL lewat perbedaan respons',
    'To confirm a TE.CL vulnerability, you would send an attack request like this:' => 'Untuk mengonfirmasi TE.CL, kirim permintaan serangan berikut:',
    'If the attack is successful, then everything from [[C0]] onwards is treated by the back-end server as belonging to the next request that is received. This will cause the subsequent "normal" request to look like this:' => 'Jika berhasil, segala sesuatu mulai dari [[C0]] dianggap awal permintaan berikutnya sehingga permintaan normal tampak seperti:',
    'Some important considerations should be kept in mind when attempting to confirm request smuggling vulnerabilities via interference with other requests:' => 'Beberapa hal penting yang perlu diperhatikan saat mengonfirmasi request smuggling:',
    'The "attack" request and the "normal" request should be sent to the server using different network connections. Sending both requests through the same connection won\'t prove that the vulnerability exists.' => 'Kirim permintaan serangan dan permintaan normal lewat dua koneksi jaringan terpisah; koneksi yang sama tidak membuktikan desinkronisasi antar-pengguna.',
    'The "attack" request and the "normal" request should use the same URL and parameter names, as far as possible. This is because many modern applications route front-end requests to different back-end servers based on the URL and parameters. Using the same URL and parameters increases the chance that the requests will be processed by the same back-end server, which is essential for the attack to work.' => 'Gunakan URL dan parameter yang sama pada kedua permintaan agar diarahkan ke server back-end yang sama oleh router front-end.',
    'When testing the "normal" request to detect any interference from the "attack" request, you are in a race with any other requests that the application is receiving at the same time, including those from other users. You should send the "normal" request immediately after the "attack" request. If the application is busy, you might need to perform multiple attempts to confirm the vulnerability.' => 'Pada aplikasi ramai, Anda berlomba dengan trafik pengguna lain sehingga mungkin perlu beberapa percobaan cepat berturut-turut.',
    'In some applications, the front-end server functions as a load balancer, and forwards requests to different back-end systems according to some load balancing algorithm. If your "attack" and "normal" requests are forwarded to different back-end systems, then the attack will fail. This is an additional reason why you might need to try several times before a vulnerability can be confirmed.' => 'Jika load balancer meneruskan ke node back-end berbeda, konfirmasi akan gagal dan perlu diulang beberapa kali.',
    'If your attack succeeds in interfering with a subsequent request, but this wasn\'t the "normal" request that you sent to detect the interference, then this means that another application user was affected by your attack. If you continue performing the test, this could have a disruptive effect on other users, and you should exercise caution.' => 'Berhati-hatilah saat menguji target produksi karena serangan bisa memengaruhi permintaan pengguna lain yang kebetulan datang bersamaan.',

    // exploiting
    "In this section, we'll describe various ways in which HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities can be exploited, depending on the intended functionality and other behavior of the application." => 'Pada bagian ini, kami membahas berbagai skenario eksploitasi HTTP request smuggling sesuai fungsi dan perilaku aplikasi.',
    'Using HTTP request smuggling to bypass front-end security controls' => 'Memakai HTTP request smuggling untuk melewati kontrol keamanan front-end',
    'In some applications, the front-end web server is used to implement some security controls, deciding whether to allow individual requests to be processed. Allowed requests are forwarded to the back-end server, where they are deemed to have passed through the front-end controls.' => 'Sebagian aplikasi memasang kontrol akses di front-end dan mempercayai penuh semua permintaan yang lolos ke back-end.',
    'For example, suppose an application uses the front-end server to implement access control restrictions, only forwarding requests if the user is authorized to access the requested URL. The back-end server then honors every request without further checking. In this situation, an HTTP request smuggling vulnerability can be used to bypass the access controls, by smuggling a request to a restricted URL.' => 'Misalnya front-end memblokir akses ke URL tertentu sementara back-end tidak mengecek ulang. Dengan menyelundupkan permintaan ke URL terlarang, pembatasan tersebut bisa dilewati.',
    'Suppose the current user is permitted to access [[C0]] but not [[C1]]. They can bypass this restriction using the following request smuggling attack:' => 'Misalkan pengguna hanya boleh mengakses [[C0]] dan bukan [[C1]]. Pembatasan dilewati dengan payload berikut:',
    'The front-end server sees two requests here, both for [[C0]], and so the requests are forwarded to the back-end server. However, the back-end server sees one request for [[C1]] and one request for [[C2]]. It assumes (as always) that the requests have passed through the front-end controls, and so grants access to the restricted URL.' => 'Front-end mengira kedua permintaan menuju [[C0]], padahal back-end membaca permintaan ke [[C1]] dan [[C2]] lalu mengizinkannya.',
    'Revealing front-end request rewriting' => 'Mengungkap penulisan ulang permintaan oleh front-end',
    'In many applications, the front-end server performs some rewriting of requests before they are forwarded to the back-end server, typically by adding some additional request headers. For example, the front-end server might:' => 'Banyak front-end menambahkan atau menulis ulang header sebelum meneruskan permintaan ke back-end, misalnya:',
    'terminate the TLS connection and add some headers describing the protocol and ciphers that were used;' => 'mengakhiri koneksi TLS lalu menambahkan header info protokol dan cipher;',
    "add an [[C0]] header containing the user's IP address;" => 'menambahkan header [[C0]] berisi alamat IP pengguna;',
    "determine the user's ID based on their session token and add a header identifying the user; or" => 'menentukan ID pengguna dari token sesi dan menyisipkan header identitas;',
    'add some sensitive information that is of interest for other attacks.' => 'atau menambah metadata sensitif lain yang berguna untuk serangan lanjutan.',
    'In some situations, if your smuggled requests are missing some headers that are normally added by the front-end server, then the back-end server might not process the requests in the normal way, resulting in smuggled requests failing to have the intended effects.' => 'Jika permintaan selundupan kehilangan header internal tersebut, back-end mungkin menolaknya.',
    'There is often a simple way to reveal exactly how the front-end server is rewriting requests. To do this, you need to perform the following steps:' => 'Anda dapat mengungkap header apa saja yang ditambahkan front-end lewat langkah berikut:',
    "Find a POST request that reflects the value of a request parameter into the application's response." => 'Cari permintaan POST yang memantulkan nilai suatu parameter ke dalam respons.',
    'Shuffle the parameters so that the reflected parameter appears last in the message body.' => 'Pindahkan parameter tersebut ke posisi paling akhir pada body.',
    'Smuggle this request to the back-end server, followed directly by a normal request whose rewritten form you want to reveal.' => 'Selundupkan permintaan ini ke back-end tepat sebelum permintaan normal lain.',
    'Suppose an application has a login function that reflects the value of the [[C0]] parameter:' => 'Misalkan fitur login memantulkan nilai parameter [[C0]]:',
    'This results in a response containing the following:' => 'Hasil responsnya memuat baris berikut:',
    'Here you can use the following request smuggling attack to reveal the rewriting that is performed by the front-end server:' => 'Kirim serangan berikut untuk membocorkan header tambahan front-end:',
    'The requests will be rewritten by the front-end server to include the additional headers, and then the back-end server will process the smuggled request and treat the rewritten second request as being the value of the [[C0]] parameter. It will then reflect this value back in the response to the second request:' => 'Front-end menulis ulang permintaan kedua beserta header tambahannya, lalu back-end menempelkannya sebagai nilai parameter [[C0]] dan memantulkannya di respons:',
    "Since the final request is being rewritten, you don't know how long it will end up. The value in the [[C0]] header in the smuggled request will determine how long the back-end server believes the request is. If you set this value too short, you will receive only part of the rewritten request; if you set it too long, the back-end server will time out waiting for the request to complete. Of course, the solution is to guess an initial value that is a bit bigger than the submitted request, and then gradually increase the value to retrieve more information, until you have everything of interest." => 'Karena panjang permintaan hasil penulisan ulang belum diketahui, sesuaikan nilai [[C0]] pada permintaan selundupan secara bertahap agar tidak terpotong terlalu pendek maupun mengalami timeout.',
    'Once you have revealed how the front-end server is rewriting requests, you can apply the necessary rewrites to your smuggled requests, to ensure they are processed in the intended way by the back-end server.' => 'Begitu format header internal terbongkar, sertakan header tersebut langsung ke permintaan selundupan Anda.',
    'Bypassing client authentication' => 'Melewati autentikasi klien',
    'As part of the TLS handshake, servers authenticate themselves with the client (usually a browser) by providing a certificate. This certificate contains their "common name" (CN), which should match their registered hostname. The client can then use this to verify that they\'re talking to a legitimate server belonging to the expected domain.' => 'Pada jabat tangan TLS, server membuktikan identitasnya lewat sertifikat beserta common name (CN).',
    "Some sites go one step further and implement a form of mutual TLS authentication, where clients must also present a certificate to the server. In this case, the client's CN is often a username or suchlike, which can be used in the back-end application logic as part of an access control mechanism, for example." => 'Sebagian situs menerapkan mutual TLS ketika klien juga wajib mengirim sertifikat berisi CN sebagai identitas akses.',
    "The component that authenticates the client typically passes the relevant details from the certificate to the application or back-end server via one or more non-standard HTTP headers. For example, front-end servers sometimes append a header containing the client's CN to any incoming requests:" => 'Komponen front-end yang memverifikasi sertifikat klien lalu meneruskan CN ke back-end lewat header HTTP khusus seperti:',
    "As these headers are supposed to be completely hidden from users, they are often implicitly trusted by back-end servers. Assuming you're able to send the right combination of headers and values, this may enable you to bypass access controls." => 'Karena header internal ini dianggap tersembunyi dari klien luar, back-end memercayainya begitu saja.',
    "In practice, this behavior isn't usually exploitable because front-end servers tend to overwrite these headers if they're already present. However, smuggled requests are hidden from the front-end altogether, so any headers they contain will be sent to the back-end unchanged." => 'Front-end biasanya menimpa header tersebut jika dikirim klien langsung, tetapi permintaan selundupan luput dari pengawasan front-end sehingga header palsu diteruskan utuh ke back-end.',
    "Capturing other users' requests" => 'Menangkap permintaan pengguna lain',
    "If the application contains any kind of functionality that allows you to store and later retrieve textual data, you can potentially use this to capture the contents of other users' requests. These may include session tokens or other sensitive data submitted by the user. Suitable functions to use as the vehicle for this attack would be comments, emails, profile descriptions, screen names, and so on." => 'Jika aplikasi memiliki fitur penyimpanan teks yang dapat dibaca ulang seperti komentar atau profil, Anda bisa menyalahgunakannya untuk merekam isi permintaan pengguna lain termasuk token sesi.',
    'To perform the attack, you need to smuggle a request that submits data to the storage function, with the parameter containing the data to store positioned last in the request. For example, suppose an application uses the following request to submit a blog post comment, which will be stored and displayed on the blog:' => 'Selundupkan permintaan ke fitur penyimpan tersebut dengan meletakkan parameter penyimpan data di urutan paling akhir. Contoh permintaan komentar normal:',
    'Now consider what would happen if you smuggle an equivalent request with an overly long [[C0]] header and the [[C1]] parameter positioned at the end of the request as follows:' => 'Kini lihat jika permintaan serupa diselundupkan dengan [[C0]] yang sengaja dibuat lebih panjang dan [[C1]] di bagian akhir:',
    "The [[C0]] header of the smuggled request indicates that the body will be 400 bytes long, but we've only sent 144 bytes. In this case, the back-end server will wait for the remaining 256 bytes before issuing the response, or else issue a timeout if this doesn't arrive quick enough. As a result, when another request is sent to the back-end server down the same connection, the first 256 bytes are effectively appended to the smuggled request as follows:" => 'Header [[C0]] menjanjikan body 400 byte tetapi baru 144 byte terkirim, sehingga 256 byte awal dari permintaan pengguna berikutnya otomatis masuk ke parameter body:',
    "As the start of the victim's request is contained in the [[C0]] parameter, this will be posted as a comment on the blog, enabling you to read it simply by visiting the relevant post." => 'Karena awal permintaan korban masuk ke parameter [[C0]], cookie dan header korban tersimpan sebagai komentar blog yang tinggal Anda baca.',
    "To capture more of the victim's request, you just need to increase the value of the smuggled request's [[C0]] header accordingly, but note that this will involve a certain amount of trial and error. If you encounter a timeout, this probably means that the [[C1]] you've specified is higher than the actual length of the victim's request. In this case, simply reduce the value until the attack works again." => 'Untuk menangkap lebih banyak bagian permintaan korban, perbesar [[C0]] secara bertahap; jika terjadi timeout, kurangi nilainya.',
    'One limitation with this technique is that it will generally only capture data up until the parameter delimiter that is applicable for the smuggled request. For URL-encoded form submissions, this will be the [[C0]] character, meaning that the content that is stored from the victim user\'s request will end at the first [[C1]], which might even appear in the query string' => 'Keterbatasannya adalah tangkapan akan terhenti begitu menemui karakter pemisah parameter seperti [[C0]] pada form URL-encoded.',
    'Using HTTP request smuggling to exploit reflected XSS' => 'Memakai HTTP request smuggling untuk mengeksploitasi reflected XSS',
    'If an application is vulnerable to HTTP request smuggling and also contains reflected XSS, you can use a request smuggling attack to hit other users of the application. This approach is superior to normal exploitation of reflected XSS in two ways:' => 'Jika aplikasi memiliki celah request smuggling sekaligus reflected XSS, Anda bisa menyerang pengguna lain dengan dua keunggulan dibanding XSS biasa:',
    "It requires no interaction with victim users. You don't need to feed them a URL and wait for them to visit it. You just smuggle a request containing the XSS payload and the next user's request that is processed by the back-end server will be hit." => 'Tidak butuh interaksi korban sama sekali; pengguna berikutnya yang memakai koneksi tersebut langsung terkena payload XSS.',
    'It can be used to exploit XSS behavior in parts of the request that cannot be trivially controlled in a normal reflected XSS attack, such as HTTP request headers.' => 'Dapat memicu XSS pada bagian permintaan yang mustahil dikontrol lewat tautan biasa, seperti header HTTP.',
    'For example, suppose an application has a reflected XSS vulnerability in the [[C0]] header. You can exploit this in a request smuggling attack as follows:' => 'Contohnya, jika header [[C0]] rentan reflected XSS, payload dapat diselundupkan seperti berikut:',
    "The next user's request will be appended to the smuggled request, and they will receive the reflected XSS payload in the response." => 'Permintaan pengguna berikutnya menempel pada permintaan selundupan sehingga ia langsung menerima respons berisi payload XSS.',
    'Using HTTP request smuggling to turn an on-site redirect into an open redirect' => 'Mengubah pengalihan internal menjadi open redirect melalui request smuggling',
    "Many applications perform on-site redirects from one URL to another and place the hostname from the request's [[C0]] header into the redirect URL. An example of this is the default behavior of Apache and IIS web servers, where a request for a folder without a trailing slash receives a redirect to the same folder including the trailing slash:" => 'Banyak server membentuk URL redirect internal dengan mengambil hostname dari header [[C0]], misalnya saat menambahkan garis miring di akhir path folder:',
    'This behavior is normally considered harmless, but it can be exploited in a request smuggling attack to redirect other users to an external domain. For example:' => 'Perilaku ini tampak wajar, namun lewat request smuggling bisa dimanfaatkan untuk mengarahkan permintaan pengguna lain ke domain eksternal:',
    "The smuggled request will trigger a redirect to the attacker's website, which will affect the next user's request that is processed by the back-end server. For example:" => 'Permintaan selundupan memicu redirect ke situs penyerang terhadap permintaan korban berikutnya:',
    "Here, the user's request was for a JavaScript file that was imported by a page on the web site. The attacker can fully compromise the victim user by returning their own JavaScript in the response." => 'Jika korban sedang memuat file JavaScript, penyerang dapat membalas dengan skrip berbahaya buatannya sendiri.',
    'Turning root-relative redirects into open redirects' => 'Mengubah redirect relatif-root menjadi open redirect',
    'In some cases, you may encounter server-level redirects that use the path to construct a root-relative URL for the [[C0]] header, for example:' => 'Terkadang server memakai path permintaan untuk menyusun URL relatif pada header [[C0]], misalnya:',
    'This can potentially still be used for an open redirect if the server lets you use a protocol-relative URL in the path:' => 'Hal ini tetap bisa menjadi open redirect jika server mengizinkan URL relatif-protokol pada path:',
    'Using HTTP request smuggling to perform web cache poisoning' => 'Memakai HTTP request smuggling untuk web cache poisoning',
    "In a variation of the preceding attack, it might be possible to exploit HTTP request smuggling to perform a web cache poisoning attack. If any part of the front-end infrastructure performs caching of content (generally for performance reasons), then it might be possible to poison the cache with the off-site redirect response. This will make the attack persistent, affecting any user who subsequently requests the affected URL." => 'Jika infrastruktur front-end menerapkan caching, respons redirect berbahaya dapat disimpan di cache sehingga menyerang seluruh pengguna berikutnya secara persisten.',
    'In this variant, the attacker sends all of the following to the front-end server:' => 'Dalam variasi ini, penyerang mengirim rangkaian berikut:',
    'The smuggled request reaches the back-end server, which responds as before with the off-site redirect. The front-end server caches this response against what it believes is the URL in the second request, which is [[C0]]:' => 'Back-end membalas dengan redirect ke domain penyerang, lalu front-end menyimpannya di cache untuk URL permintaan kedua yaitu [[C0]]:',
    "From this point onwards, when other users request this URL, they receive the redirection to the attacker's web site." => 'Mulai saat itu, setiap pengguna yang meminta URL tersebut langsung diarahkan ke situs penyerang.',
    'Using HTTP request smuggling to perform web cache deception' => 'Memakai HTTP request smuggling untuk web cache deception',
    'In yet another variant of the attack, you can leverage HTTP request smuggling to perform web cache deception. This works in a similar way to the web cache poisoning attack but with a different purpose.' => 'Variasi lain adalah memakai request smuggling untuk web cache deception dengan mekanisme serupa tetapi tujuan berbeda.',
    'In <strong>web cache poisoning</strong>, the attacker causes the application to store some malicious content in the cache, and this content is served from the cache to other application users.' => 'Pada <strong>web cache poisoning</strong>, penyerang membuat cache menyimpan konten berbahaya untuk disajikan ke pengguna lain.',
    'In <strong>web cache deception</strong>, the attacker causes the application to store some sensitive content belonging to another user in the cache, and the attacker then retrieves this content from the cache.' => 'Pada <strong>web cache deception</strong>, penyerang memicu cache menyimpan data sensitif milik korban lalu mengambil data itu dari cache.',
    'In this variant, the attacker smuggles a request that returns some sensitive user-specific content. For example:' => 'Penyerang menyelundupkan permintaan ke endpoint yang menampilkan data privat pengguna:',
    'The next request from another user that is forwarded to the back-end server will be appended to the smuggled request, including session cookies and other headers. For example:' => 'Permintaan pengguna berikutnya beserta cookie sesinya menempel di belakang permintaan selundupan tersebut:',
    "The back-end server responds to this request in the normal way. The URL in the request is for the user's private messages and the request is processed in the context of the victim user's session. The front-end server caches this response against what it believes is the URL in the second request, which is [[C0]]:" => 'Back-end merespons halaman privat korban, lalu front-end menyimpannya ke cache sebagai respons milik [[C0]]:',
    'The attacker then visits the static URL and receives the sensitive content that is returned from the cache.' => 'Penyerang tinggal mengakses URL statis tersebut untuk mengambil data sensitif korban dari cache.',
    "An important caveat here is that the attacker doesn't know the URL against which the sensitive content will be cached, since this will be whatever URL the victim user happened to be requesting when the smuggled request took effect. The attacker might need to fetch a large number of static URLs to discover the captured content." => 'Catatannya, penyerang tidak selalu tahu URL statis mana yang tepat diakses korban sehingga mungkin perlu memeriksa beberapa URL statis dari cache.',

    // advanced
    'Advanced request smuggling' => 'Request smuggling lanjutan',
    "In this section, we'll build on the concepts you've learned so far and teach you some more advanced HTTP request smuggling techniques. We'll also cover a variety of HTTP/2-based attacks that are made possible thanks to [[A0]]Burp's unique HTTP/2-testing capabilities[[/A0]]. Don't worry if you're new to HTTP/2 - we'll cover all the essentials as we go." => 'Pada bagian ini, kami memperdalam teknik request smuggling lanjutan termasuk berbagai vektor berbasis HTTP/2 menggunakan [[A0]]lab praktik terkait[[/A0]]. Semua dasar HTTP/2 dijelaskan seiring pembahasan.',
    'In particular, we\'ll look at:' => 'Secara khusus, kita akan membahas:',
    'How common HTTP/2 implementations enable a range of [[A0]]powerful new vectors for request smuggling[[/A0]], making a number of previously secure sites vulnerable to these kinds of attacks.' => 'Bagaimana implementasi HTTP/2 umum membuka berbagai [[A0]]vektor request smuggling baru[[/A0]] pada situs yang sebelumnya aman.',
    'How you can use request smuggling to persistently [[A0]]poison the response queue[[/A0]], effectively enabling full-site takeover.' => 'Cara menggunakan request smuggling untuk [[A0]]meracuni antrean respons[[/A0]] secara persisten hingga mengambil alih situs.',
    "How you can use HTTP/2-exclusive inputs to construct high-severity exploits [[A0]]even when the target doesn't reuse the connection[[/A0]] between the front-end and back-end servers at all." => 'Cara memakai input khusus HTTP/2 untuk melancarkan eksploitasi [[A0]]meski server tidak memakai ulang koneksi back-end[[/A0]].',
    "To help you practice what you've learned, we've provided deliberately vulnerable labs throughout. These are based on real-world vulnerabilities first presented at Black Hat USA 2021 by our Director of Research, [[A0]]James Kettle[[/A0]]." => 'Untuk berlatih, tersedia lab rentan yang dibuat berdasarkan temuan nyata dalam riset keamanan HTTP/2 oleh [[A0]]tim peneliti[[/A0]].',
    'HTTP/2: The Sequel Is Always Worse' => 'HTTP/2: Sekuel yang Selalu Lebih Buruk',
    'HTTP/2 request smuggling' => 'Request smuggling HTTP/2',
    "In this section, we'll show you how, contrary to popular belief, implementing HTTP/2 has actually made many websites more vulnerable to request smuggling, even if they were previously safe from these kinds of attacks." => 'Di bagian ini, kami menunjukkan mengapa penerapan HTTP/2 justru membuat banyak situs lebih rentan terhadap request smuggling.',
    'HTTP/2 message length' => 'Panjang pesan HTTP/2',
    "Request smuggling is fundamentally about exploiting discrepancies between how different servers interpret the length of a request. HTTP/2 introduces a single, robust mechanism for doing this, which has long been thought to make it inherently immune to request smuggling." => 'Request smuggling memanfaatkan perbedaan tafsir panjang pesan. HTTP/2 menghadirkan satu mekanisme tunggal yang sempat diyakini membuatnya kebal.',
    'Although you won\'t see this in Burp, HTTP/2 messages are sent over the wire as a series of separate "frames". Each frame is preceded by an explicit length field, which tells the server exactly how many bytes to read in. Therefore, the length of the request is the sum of its frame lengths.' => 'Pesan HTTP/2 dikirim sebagai rangkaian frame biner yang masing-masing diawali field panjang eksplisit, sehingga total panjang permintaan adalah jumlah panjang frame-nya.',
    'In theory, this mechanism means there is no opportunity for an attacker to introduce the ambiguity required for request smuggling, as long as the website uses HTTP/2 end to end. In the wild, however, this is often not the case due to the widespread but dangerous practice of HTTP/2 downgrading.' => 'Secara teori mekanisme ini mencegah ambiguitas bila HTTP/2 dipakai end-to-end. Namun di lapangan, praktik HTTP/2 downgrading masih sangat sering ditemukan.',
    'HTTP/2 downgrading' => 'Downgrading HTTP/2',
    'HTTP/2 downgrading is the process of rewriting HTTP/2 requests using HTTP/1 syntax to generate an equivalent HTTP/1 request. Web servers and reverse proxies often do this in order to offer HTTP/2 support to clients while communicating with back-end servers that only speak HTTP/1. This practice is a prerequisite for many of the attacks covered in this section.' => 'HTTP/2 downgrading adalah proses menerjemahkan permintaan HTTP/2 menjadi sintaks HTTP/1 untuk diteruskan ke server back-end yang hanya mendukung HTTP/1.',
    'What is HTTP/2 downgrading?' => 'Apa itu HTTP/2 downgrading?',
    "As HTTP/2 is a binary protocol, we've used some artistic license to represent HTTP/2 messages in a human-readable format throughout these materials:" => 'Karena HTTP/2 adalah protokol biner, materi ini menampilkan pesannya dalam format teks agar mudah dibaca:',
    'We display each message as a single entity, rather than separate "frames".' => 'Setiap pesan ditampilkan utuh, bukan terpecah per frame.',
    'We display the headers using plain text name and value fields.' => 'Header ditampilkan sebagai pasangan nama dan nilai teks biasa.',
    'We prefix pseudo-header names with a colon to help differentiate them from normal headers.' => 'Nama pseudo-header diawali tanda titik dua untuk membedakannya dari header normal.',
    "This closely resembles the way [[A0]]Burp represents HTTP/2 messages[[/A0]] in the Inspector, but note that they don't actually look like this on the wire." => 'Tampilan ini menyerupai inspektor pesan di [[A0]]lab praktik[[/A0]], bukan bentuk biner mentah di jaringan.',
    'H2.CL vulnerabilities' => 'Kerentanan H2.CL',
    "HTTP/2 requests don't have to specify their length explicitly in a header. During downgrading, this means front-end servers often add an HTTP/1 [[C0]] header, deriving its value using [[A0]]HTTP/2's built-in length mechanism[[/A0]]. Interestingly, HTTP/2 requests can also include their own [[C1]] header. In this case, some front-end servers will simply reuse this value in the resulting HTTP/1 request." => 'Permintaan HTTP/2 tidak wajib menyertakan header panjang, sehingga front-end biasanya menambahkan header [[C0]] HTTP/1 dari [[A0]]panjang frame bawaan HTTP/2[[/A0]]. Namun jika pengirim menyertakan [[C1]] sendiri, sebagian front-end langsung menyalin nilainya saat downgrade.',
    "The spec dictates that any [[C0]] header in an HTTP/2 request must match the length calculated using the built-in mechanism, but this isn't always validated properly before downgrading. As a result, it may be possible to smuggle requests by injecting a misleading [[C1]] header. Although the front-end will use the implicit HTTP/2 length to determine where the request ends, the HTTP/1 back-end has to refer to the [[C2]] header derived from your injected one, resulting in a desync." => 'Jika validasi kecocokan [[C0]] gagal dilakukan saat downgrade, header [[C1]] palsu diteruskan ke back-end HTTP/1 sebagai [[C2]] sementara front-end tetap berpatokan pada panjang frame HTTP/2, menimbulkan desync.',
    "When performing some request smuggling attacks, you will want headers from the victim's request to be appended to your smuggled prefix. However, these can interfere with your attack in some cases, resulting in duplicate header errors and suchlike. In the example above, we've mitigated this by including a trailing parameter and a [[C0]] header in the smuggled prefix. By using a [[C1]] header that is slightly longer than the body, the victim's request will still be appended to your smuggled prefix but will be truncated before the headers." => 'Menambahkan parameter di akhir prefix beserta [[C0]] sedikit lebih panjang memastikan permintaan korban ikut menempel sebagai isi parameter tanpa memicu error duplikasi header lewat [[C1]].',
    'H2.TE vulnerabilities' => 'Kerentanan H2.TE',
    'Chunked transfer encoding is incompatible with HTTP/2 and the spec recommends that any [[C0]] header you try to inject should be stripped or the request blocked entirely. If the front-end server fails to do this, and subsequently downgrades the request for an HTTP/1 back-end that does support chunked encoding, this can also enable request smuggling attacks.' => 'Chunked transfer encoding tidak kompatibel dengan HTTP/2 sehingga [[C0]] seharusnya dihapus atau ditolak. Jika front-end meloloskannya saat downgrade ke back-end HTTP/1, terjadilah request smuggling H2.TE.',
    'If a website is vulnerable to either H2.CL or H2.TE request smuggling, you can potentially leverage this behavior to perform the same attacks that we covered in our [[A0]]previous request smuggling labs[[/A0]].' => 'Celah H2.CL atau H2.TE dapat dipakai melancarkan seluruh serangan pada [[A0]]materi request smuggling sebelumnya[[/A0]].',
    'Hidden HTTP/2 support' => 'Dukungan HTTP/2 tersembunyi',
    'Browsers and other clients, including Burp, typically only use HTTP/2 to communicate with servers that explicitly advertise support for it via ALPN as part of the TLS handshake.' => 'Klien umumnya hanya memakai HTTP/2 bila server mengumumkannya lewat ALPN saat TLS handshake.',
    'Some servers support HTTP/2 but fail to declare this properly due to misconfiguration. In such cases, it can appear as though the server only supports HTTP/1.1 because clients default to this as a fallback option. As a result, testers may overlook viable HTTP/2 attack surface and miss protocol-level issues, such as the examples of HTTP/2 downgrade-based request smuggling that we covered above.' => 'Sebagian server sebenarnya mendukung HTTP/2 tetapi lupa mengumumkannya di ALPN karena salah konfigurasi, sehingga terlewat saat pengujian.',
    'To force Burp Repeater to use HTTP/2 so that you can test for this misconfiguration manually:' => 'Untuk memaksa pengujian HTTP/2 secara manual meskipun tidak diumumkan ALPN:',
    'From the <strong>Settings</strong> dialog, go to <strong>Tools &gt; Repeater</strong>.' => 'Buka pengaturan alat pengujian protokol Anda.',
    'Under <strong>Connections</strong>, enable the <strong>Allow HTTP/2 ALPN override</strong> option.' => 'Aktifkan opsi pengabaian negosiasi ALPN HTTP/2.',
    'In Repeater, go to the <strong>Inspector</strong> panel and expand the <strong>Request attributes</strong> section.' => 'Pilih panel atribut permintaan pada pengirim permintaan.',
    'Use the switch to set the <strong>Protocol</strong> to <strong>HTTP/2</strong>. Burp will now send all requests on this tab using HTTP/2, regardless of whether the server advertises support for this.' => 'Ubah protokol ke HTTP/2 sehingga seluruh permintaan pada sesi uji dikirim via HTTP/2.',
    "If you're using Burp Suite Professional, Burp Scanner automatically detects instances of hidden HTTP/2 support." => 'Pemindai kerentanan otomatis modern biasanya juga mendeteksi dukungan HTTP/2 tersembunyi.',
    'Response queue poisoning' => 'Response queue poisoning',
    'Response queue poisoning is a powerful request smuggling attack that enables you to steal arbitrary responses intended for other users, potentially compromising their accounts and even the entire site.' => 'Response queue poisoning adalah serangan request smuggling tingkat lanjut untuk mencuri respons HTTP milik pengguna lain hingga membajak akun dan seluruh situs.',
    'Request smuggling via CRLF injection' => 'Request smuggling melalui injeksi CRLF',
    "Even if websites take steps to prevent basic H2.CL or H2.TE attacks, such as validating the [[C0]] or stripping any [[C1]] headers, HTTP/2's binary format enables some novel ways to bypass these kinds of front-end measures." => 'Walau server sudah memvalidasi [[C0]] atau menghapus [[C1]], format biner HTTP/2 membuka cara baru melewati filter front-end.',
    'In HTTP/1, you can sometimes exploit discrepancies between how servers handle standalone newline ([[C0]]) characters to smuggle prohibited headers. If the back-end treats this as a delimiter, but the front-end server does not, some front-end servers will fail to detect the second header at all.' => 'Di HTTP/1, karakter baris baru tunggal [[C0]] kadang ditafsirkan sebagai pemisah oleh back-end sementara front-end mengabaikannya.',
    "This discrepancy doesn't exist with the handling of a full CRLF ([[C0]]) sequence because all HTTP/1 servers agree that this terminates the header." => 'Perbedaan ini tidak terjadi pada [[C0]] penuh karena semua server HTTP/1 sepakat menggunakannya sebagai akhir baris header.',
    'On the other hand, as HTTP/2 messages are binary rather than text-based, the boundaries of each header are based on explicit, predetermined offsets rather than delimiter characters. This means that [[C0]] no longer has any special significance within a header value and, therefore, can be included <strong>inside</strong> the value itself without causing the header to be split:' => 'Sebaliknya, pada HTTP/2 batas header ditentukan oleh panjang biner eksplisit sehingga [[C0]] dapat disisipkan <strong>di dalam</strong> nilai header tanpa membelah header di front-end:',
    'This may seem relatively harmless on its own, but when this is rewritten as an HTTP/1 request, the [[C0]] will once again be interpreted as a header delimiter. As a result, an HTTP/1 back-end server would see two distinct headers:' => 'Namun saat di-downgrade ke HTTP/1, [[C0]] tersebut kembali ditafsirkan sebagai pemisah baris header sehingga back-end melihat dua header berbeda:',
    'Additional HTTP/2-exclusive attack vectors' => 'Vektor serangan eksklusif HTTP/2 tambahan',
    'HTTP/2 request splitting' => 'Pemecahan permintaan HTTP/2',
    "When we looked at [[A0]]response queue poisoning[[/A0]], you learned how to split a single HTTP request into exactly two complete requests on the back-end. In the example we looked at, the split occurred inside the message body, but when HTTP/2 downgrading is in play, you can also cause this split to occur in the headers instead." => 'Berbeda dari [[A0]]response queue poisoning[[/A0]] di dalam body, downgrading HTTP/2 memungkinkan pemecahan satu permintaan menjadi dua permintaan utuh tepat di bagian header.',
    "This approach is more versatile because you aren't dependent on using request methods that are allowed to contain a body. For example, you can even use a [[C0]] request:" => 'Teknik ini lebih fleksibel karena tidak membutuhkan body dan bisa memakai metode [[C0]]:',
    "This is also useful in cases where the [[C0]] is validated and the back-end doesn't support chunked encoding." => 'Cara ini juga efektif ketika [[C0]] divalidasi ketat dan back-end tidak mendukung chunked encoding.',
    'Accounting for front-end rewriting' => 'Memperhitungkan penulisan ulang oleh front-end',
    'To split a request in the headers, you need to understand how the request is rewritten by the front-end server and account for this when adding any HTTP/1 headers manually. Otherwise, one of the requests may be missing mandatory headers.' => 'Saat memecah permintaan di header, pahami cara front-end menambahkan header wajib seperti Host agar tidak ada permintaan yang cacat.',
    'For example, you need to ensure that both requests received by the back-end contain a [[C0]] header. Front-end servers typically strip the [[C1]] pseudo-header and replace it with a new HTTP/1 [[C2]] header during downgrading. There are different approaches for doing this, which can influence where you need to position the [[C3]] header that you\'re injecting.' => 'Kedua permintaan harus memiliki header [[C0]]. Saat downgrade, front-end mengganti pseudo-header [[C1]] dengan [[C2]], yang memengaruhi letak penyisipan [[C3]].',
    'Consider the following request:' => 'Perhatikan permintaan berikut:',
    'During rewriting, some front-end servers append the new [[C0]] header to the end of the current list of headers. As far as an HTTP/2 front-end is concerned, this after the [[C1]] header. Note that this is also after the point at which the request will be split on the back-end. This means that the first request would have no [[C2]] header at all, while the smuggled request would have two. In this case, you need to position your injected [[C3]] header so that it ends up in the first request once the split occurs:' => 'Jika front-end menaruh [[C0]] hasil konversi [[C1]] di akhir daftar header, header tersebut jatuh ke permintaan kedua dan permintaan pertama kehilangan [[C2]]. Sisipkan [[C3]] Anda sebelum titik pemecahan agar permintaan pertama tetap valid:',
    'You will also need to adjust the positioning of any internal headers that you want to inject in a similar manner.' => 'Sesuaikan posisi header lain yang ingin Anda suntikkan dengan pola yang sama.',
    "In the example above, we've split the request in a way that triggers [[A0]]response queue poisoning[[/A0]], but you can also smuggle prefixes for classic request smuggling attacks in this way. In this case, your injected headers may clash with the headers in the request that is appended to your prefix on the back-end, resulting in duplicate header errors or causing the request to be terminated in the wrong place. To mitigate this, you can include a trailing body parameter in the smuggled prefix along with a [[C0]] header that is slightly longer than the body. The victim's request will still be appended to your smuggled prefix but will be truncated before the headers." => 'Pemecahan di header bisa memicu [[A0]]response queue poisoning[[/A0]] maupun menyelundupkan prefix klasik; sertakan parameter penampung di akhir body dengan [[C0]] sedikit lebih panjang untuk mencegah bentrok header.',
    'HTTP request tunnelling' => 'HTTP request tunnelling',
    "Many of the request smuggling attacks we've covered so far are only possible because the same connection between the front-end and back-end server is used for handling multiple requests. HTTP request tunnelling provides a way to craft high-severity exploits even when there is no connection reuse at all." => 'HTTP request tunnelling memungkinkan eksploitasi tingkat lanjut bahkan ketika tidak ada pemakaian ulang koneksi antara front-end dan back-end.',
    '0.CL request smuggling' => 'Request smuggling 0.CL',
    '0.CL desync attacks occur when the front-end server ignores a [[C0]] header that the back-end server processes. This scenario was long considered unexploitable due to connection deadlocks between servers.' => 'Serangan desync 0.CL terjadi ketika front-end mengabaikan header [[C0]] yang justru diproses back-end, skenario yang lama dianggap mustahil karena deadlock koneksi.',
    'However, by combining a 0.CL attack with an early-response gadget - a technique to make the back-end respond before receiving the complete request body - attackers can break the deadlock, then use a double desync to build a full exploit. This breakthrough enables the exploitation of 0.CL scenarios.' => 'Namun dengan menggabungkan gadget respons dini dari back-end dan desync ganda, kebuntuan koneksi dapat dipecahkan untuk membangun eksploitasi penuh.',
    'For more technical details, see the accompanying whitepaper: [[A0]]HTTP/1.1 Must Die[[/A0]].' => 'Simak rincian teknisnya dalam makalah riset: [[A0]]HTTP/1.1 Must Die[[/A0]].',

    // http2-downgrading
    'As HTTP/2 is still relatively new, web servers that support it often still have to communicate with legacy back-end infrastructure that only speaks HTTP/1. As a result, it has become common practice for front-end servers rewrite each incoming HTTP/2 request using HTTP/1 syntax, effectively generating its HTTP/1 equivalent. This "downgraded" request is then forwarded to the relevant back-end server instead.' => 'Agar server modern berdukungan HTTP/2 tetap kompatibel dengan back-end lama berbasis HTTP/1, front-end sering menerjemahkan setiap permintaan HTTP/2 ke bentuk HTTP/1 sebelum meneruskannya.',
    'When the HTTP/1-speaking back-end issues a response, the front-end server reverses this process to generate the HTTP/2 response that it returns to the client.' => 'Ketika back-end HTTP/1 mengirim balasan, front-end mengonversinya kembali menjadi respons HTTP/2 ke klien.',
    'This works because each version of the protocol is fundamentally just a different way of representing the same information. Each item in an HTTP/1 message has an approximate equivalent in HTTP/2.' => 'Konversi dua arah ini berjalan lancar karena tiap elemen HTTP/1 memiliki padanan langsung di HTTP/2.',
    'As a result, it\'s relatively straightforward for servers to convert these requests and responses between the two protocols. In fact, this is how Burp is able to [[A0]]display HTTP/2 messages in the message editor using HTTP/1 syntax[[/A0]].' => 'Konversi ini pula yang memudahkan alat pengujian [[A0]]menampilkan pesan HTTP/2 dalam format HTTP/1[[/A0]].',
    'HTTP/2 downgrading is extremely widespread and is even the default behavior for a number of popular reverse proxy services. In some cases, there isn\'t even an option to disable it.' => 'Praktik HTTP/2 downgrading sangat umum dan sering aktif secara default pada berbagai reverse proxy populer.',
    'What risks are associated with HTTP/2 downgrading?' => 'Apa risiko dari HTTP/2 downgrading?',
    'HTTP/2 downgrading can expose websites to request smuggling attacks, even though HTTP/2 itself is generally considered immune when used end to end.' => 'HTTP/2 downgrading dapat mengekspos situs terhadap request smuggling meskipun HTTP/2 sendiri aman bila dijalankan end-to-end.',
    'HTTP/2\'s built-in length mechanism means that, when HTTP downgrading is used, there are potentially three different ways to specify the length of the same request, which is the basis of all request smuggling attacks.' => 'Mekanisme panjang bawaan HTTP/2 ditambah header HTTP/1 saat downgrade membuka hingga tiga tafsir panjang permintaan yang menjadi akar request smuggling.',

    // http2-exclusive-vectors
    'HTTP/2-exclusive vectors' => 'Vektor eksklusif HTTP/2',
    'Due to the fact that HTTP/2 is a binary protocol rather than text-based, there are a number of potential vectors that are impossible to construct in HTTP/1 due to the limitations of its syntax.' => 'Karena HTTP/2 berbentuk biner dan bukan teks, muncul sejumlah vektor injeksi yang mustahil dikirim lewat sintaks HTTP/1.',
    "We've already seen how you can [[A0]]inject CRLF sequences into a header value[[/A0]]. In this section, we'll give you an idea of some of the other HTTP/2-exclusive vectors you can use to inject payloads. Although these kinds of requests are officially prohibited by the HTTP/2 specification, some servers fail to validate and block them effectively." => 'Selain [[A0]]injeksi CRLF di nilai header[[/A0]], bagian ini mengulas vektor eksklusif HTTP/2 lain yang tetap lolos di server dengan validasi lemah.',
    "It's only possible to perform these attacks using the [[A0]]specialized HTTP/2 features[[/A0]] in Burp's Inspector panel." => 'Serangan ini membutuhkan kemampuan penyuntingan frame/pseudo-header HTTP/2 secara langsung seperti di [[A0]]daftar lab praktik[[/A0]].',
    'Injecting via header names' => 'Injeksi melalui nama header',
    "In HTTP/1, it's not possible for a header name to contain a colon because this character is used to indicate the end of the name to parsers. This is not the case in HTTP/2." => 'Di HTTP/1 nama header tidak boleh berisi titik dua karena menjadi pemisah nama dan nilai, tetapi batasan teks itu tidak berlaku secara biner di HTTP/2.',
    "By combining colons with [[C0]] characters, you may be able to use an HTTP/2 header's name field to sneak other headers past front-end filters. These will then be interpreted as separate headers on the back-end once the request is rewritten using HTTP/1 syntax:" => 'Dengan menggabungkan titik dua dan [[C0]] di dalam nama header HTTP/2, Anda bisa menyelundupkan header tambahan ke back-end saat di-downgrade:',
    'Injecting via pseudo-headers' => 'Injeksi melalui pseudo-header',
    'HTTP/2 doesn\'t use a request line or status line. Instead, this data is passed via a series of "pseudo-headers" on the front of the request. In text-based representations of HTTP/2 messages, these are commonly prefixed with a colon to help differentiate them from normal headers. There five pseudo-headers in total:' => 'HTTP/2 mengganti baris permintaan dengan lima "pseudo-header" berawalan tanda titik dua:',
    '[[C0]] - The request method.' => '[[C0]] untuk metode permintaan HTTP.',
    '[[C0]] - The request path. Note that this includes the query string.' => '[[C0]] untuk path permintaan beserta query string.',
    '[[C0]] - Roughly equivalent to the HTTP/1 [[C1]] header.' => '[[C0]] yang kira-kira setara dengan header [[C1]] pada HTTP/1.',
    '[[C0]] - The request scheme, typically [[C1]] or [[C2]].' => '[[C0]] untuk skema permintaan, biasanya [[C1]] atau [[C2]].',
    '[[C0]] - The response status code (not used in requests).' => '[[C0]] untuk kode status respons (tidak dipakai pada permintaan).',
    'When websites downgrade requests to HTTP/1, they use the values of some of these pseudo-headers to dynamically construct the request line. This enables some interesting new ways of constructing attacks.' => 'Saat downgrade ke HTTP/1, nilai pseudo-header dipakai menyusun baris permintaan secara dinamis sehingga membuka berbagai vektor injeksi.',
    'Supplying an ambiguous host' => 'Menyuplai host yang ambigu',
    "Although the HTTP/1 [[C0]] header is effectively replaced by the [[C1]] pseudo-header in HTTP/2, you're still allowed to send a [[C2]] header in the request as well." => 'Walaupun [[C0]] digantikan oleh pseudo-header [[C1]], permintaan HTTP/2 masih boleh menyertakan header [[C2]] tambahan.',
    'In some cases, this may result in two [[C0]] headers occurring in the rewritten HTTP/1 request, which opens up another possibility for bypassing front-end "duplicate [[C1]] header" filters, for example. This potentially makes the site vulnerable to a range of [[A0]][[C2]] header attacks[[/A0]] to which it may otherwise have been immune.' => 'Akibatnya permintaan HTTP/1 hasil downgrade memuat dua header [[C0]], melewati filter duplikasi [[C1]] dan membuka peluang [[A0]]serangan header [[C2]][[/A0]].',
    'Supplying an ambiguous path' => 'Menyuplai path yang ambigu',
    "Trying to send a request with an ambiguous path is not possible in HTTP/1 due to how the request line is parsed. But as the path in HTTP/2 is specified using a pseudo-header, it's now possible to send a request with two distinct paths as follows:" => 'Di HTTP/2 path dikirim via pseudo-header sehingga Anda dapat menyertakan dua nilai path berbeda sekaligus:',
    "If there is a discrepancy between which path is validated by the website's access controls and which path is used to route the request, this may enable you to access endpoints that would otherwise be off limits." => 'Jika komponen keamanan memeriksa salah satu path sedangkan router memakai path yang lain, endpoint terbatas dapat ditembus.',
    'Injecting a full request line' => 'Menyuntikkan baris permintaan lengkap',
    'During downgrading, the value of the [[C0]] pseudo-header will be written to the very beginning of the resulting HTTP/1 request. If the server allows you to include spaces in the [[C1]] value, you may be able to inject an entirely different request line as follows:' => 'Nilai [[C0]] ditulis di awal baris permintaan HTTP/1. Jika spasi diizinkan pada [[C1]], seluruh baris permintaan dapat disuntikkan:',
    'As long as the server also tolerates the arbitrary trailing characters in the request line, this provides another means of creating a request with an ambiguous path.' => 'Selama server menoleransi karakter sisa di akhir baris permintaan, teknik ini efektif membelokkan path.',
    'Injecting a URL prefix' => 'Menyuntikkan prefiks URL',
    'Another interesting feature of HTTP/2 is the ability to explicitly specify a scheme in the request itself using the [[C0]] pseudo-header. Although this will ordinarily just contain [[C1]] or [[C2]], you may be able to include arbitrary values.' => 'Pseudo-header [[C0]] normalnya berisi [[C1]] atau [[C2]], tetapi server yang tidak memvalidasinya memungkinkan injeksi prefiks URL sembarang.',
    'This can be useful when the server uses the [[C0]] header to dynamically generate a URL, for example. In this case, you could add a prefix to the URL or even override it completely by pushing the real URL into the query string:' => 'Jika server memakai [[C0]] untuk membangun URL redirect atau cache, prefiks URL asli dapat ditimpa sepenuhnya:',
    'Injecting newlines into pseudo-headers' => 'Menyuntikkan newline ke dalam pseudo-header',
    'When injecting into the [[C0]] or [[C1]] pseudo-headers, you need to make sure that the resulting HTTP/1 request still has a valid request line.' => 'Saat menyuntikkan CRLF ke [[C0]] atau [[C1]], pastikan baris permintaan HTTP/1 tetap terbentuk sah sebelum baris baru.',
    'As the [[C0]] terminates the request line in HTTP/1, simply adding [[C1]] part way through will just break the request. After downgrading, the rewritten request must contain the following sequence prior to the first [[C2]] that you inject:' => 'Karena [[C0]] mengakhiri baris permintaan di HTTP/1, menyisipkan [[C1]] tanpa pola lengkap akan merusak sintaks sebelum [[C2]]:',
    'Just visualize where your injection falls within this sequence and include all of the remaining parts accordingly. For example, when injecting into the [[C0]], you need to add a space and [[C1]] before the [[C2]] as follows:' => 'Lengkapi sisa elemen baris permintaan di payload Anda, misalnya menambahkan spasi dan [[C1]] sebelum [[C2]] saat menyuntikkan ke [[C0]]:',
    "Notice that in this case, we've also added an arbitrary trailing header to catch the space and protocol that were added automatically during rewriting." => 'Header penutup ditambahkan di akhir agar spasi dan versi protokol bawaan front-end tidak merusak permintaan selundupan.',

    // request-tunnelling
    "Many of the request smuggling attacks we've covered are only possible because the same connection between the front-end and back-end handles multiple requests. Although some servers will reuse the connection for any requests, others have stricter policies." => 'Banyak serangan request smuggling bergantung pada koneksi front-end ke back-end yang dipakai ulang oleh banyak pengguna, namun sebagian server menerapkan kebijakan koneksi yang lebih ketat.',
    "For example, some servers only allow requests originating from the same IP address or the same client to reuse the connection. Others won't reuse the connection at all, which limits what you can achieve through classic request smuggling as you have no obvious way to influence other users' traffic." => 'Beberapa server membatasi pemakaian ulang koneksi hanya untuk IP atau klien yang sama, atau tidak memakainya ulang sama sekali.',
    "Although you can't poison the socket to interfere with other users' requests, you can still send a single request that will elicit two responses from the back-end. This potentially enables you to hide a request and its matching response from the front-end altogether." => 'Meski tidak bisa meracuni trafik pengguna lain, Anda tetap dapat mengirim satu permintaan yang memicu dua respons dari back-end sehingga menyembunyikan permintaan kedua dari pemeriksaan front-end.',
    'You can use this technique to bypass front-end security measures that may otherwise prevent you from sending certain requests. In fact, even some mechanisms designed specifically to prevent request smuggling attacks fail to stop request tunnelling.' => 'Teknik ini berguna melewati kontrol keamanan front-end maupun mekanisme anti-smuggling yang hanya mengandalkan pemisahan koneksi.',
    'Tunneling requests to the back-end in this way offers a more limited form of request smuggling, but it can still lead to high-severity exploits in the right hands.' => 'Meskipun lebih terbatas daripada smuggled prefix lintas pengguna, request tunnelling tetap dapat menghasilkan eksploitasi berdampak tinggi.',
    'Request tunnelling with HTTP/2' => 'Request tunnelling dengan HTTP/2',
    "Request tunnelling is possible with both HTTP/1 and HTTP/2 but is considerably more difficult to detect in HTTP/1-only environments. Due to the way persistent ([[C0]]) connections work in HTTP/1, even if you do receive two responses, this doesn't necessarily confirm that the request was successfully smuggled." => 'Request tunnelling lebih sulit dideteksi di lingkungan HTTP/1 murni karena perilaku koneksi persisten ([[C0]]).',
    'In HTTP/2 on the other hand, each "stream" should only ever contain a single request and response. If you receive an HTTP/2 response with what appears to be an HTTP/1 response in the body, you can be confident that you\'ve successfully tunneled a second request.' => 'Di HTTP/2, satu stream seharusnya hanya berisi satu permintaan dan satu respons. Jika respons HTTP/2 justru memuat balasan HTTP/1 di dalam body-nya, Anda berhasil melakukan tunnelling.',
    'Leaking internal headers via HTTP/2 request tunnelling' => 'Membocorkan header internal melalui HTTP/2 request tunnelling',
    "When request tunnelling is your only option, you won't be able to leak internal headers using the technique we covered in [[A0]]one of our earlier labs[[/A0]], but HTTP/2 downgrading enables an alternative solution." => 'Saat tidak ada pemakaian ulang koneksi, teknik membocorkan header pada [[A0]]lab sebelumnya[[/A0]] tidak berlaku, tetapi HTTP/2 downgrading menyediakan jalan alternatif.',
    "You can potentially trick the front-end into appending the internal headers inside what will become a body parameter on the back-end. Let's say we send a request that looks something like this:" => 'Front-end dapat diakali untuk menaruh header internal tepat ke dalam parameter body yang dibaca back-end seperti berikut:',
    "In this case, both the front-end and back-end agree that there is only one request. What's interesting is that they can be made to disagree on where the headers end." => 'Front-end dan back-end sama-sama menganggap hanya ada satu permintaan, namun berbeda pendapat di mana bagian header berakhir.',
    'The front-end sees everything we\'ve injected as part of a header, so adds any new headers after the trailing [[C0]] string. On the other hand, the back-end sees the [[C1]] sequence and thinks this is the end of the headers. The [[C2]] string, along with the internal headers, are treated as part of the body. The result is a [[C3]] parameter with the internal headers as its value.' => 'Front-end menaruh header tambahan tepat sesudah [[C0]], sedangkan back-end menghentikan pembacaan header pada [[C1]] sehingga [[C2]] dan seluruh header internal masuk sebagai nilai parameter [[C3]].',
    'Blind request tunnelling' => 'Blind request tunnelling',
    'Some front-end servers read in all the data they receive from the back-end. This means that if you successfully tunnel a request, they will potentially forward both responses to the client, with the response to the tunnelled request nested inside the body of the main response.' => 'Sebagian front-end meneruskan semua byte dari back-end sehingga respons permintaan terowongan muncul langsung di dalam body respons utama.',
    "Other front-end servers only read in the number of bytes specified in the [[C0]] header of the response, so only the first response is forwarded to the client. This results in a blind request tunnelling vulnerability because you won't be able to see the response to your tunnelled request." => 'Front-end lain hanya membaca tepat sebanyak nilai header [[C0]] respons pertama sehingga respons kedua tidak tampak dan bersifat blind.',
    'Non-blind request tunnelling using HEAD' => 'Request tunnelling non-blind menggunakan HEAD',
    'Blind request tunnelling can be tricky to exploit, but you can occasionally make these vulnerabilities non-blind by using [[C0]] requests.' => 'Blind request tunnelling terkadang dapat diubah menjadi non-blind menggunakan permintaan [[C0]].',
    "Responses to [[C0]] requests often contain a [[C1]] header even though they don't have a body of their own. This normally refers to the length of the resource that would be returned by a [[C2]] request to the same endpoint. Some front-end servers fail to account for this and attempt to read in the number of bytes specified in the header regardless. If you successfully tunnel a request past a front-end server that does this, this behavior may cause it to over-read the response from the back-end. As a result, the response you receive may contain bytes from the start of the response to your tunnelled request." => 'Respons [[C0]] sering mencantumkan [[C1]] milik permintaan [[C2]] meski tidak memiliki body. Jika front-end tetap membaca byte sebanyak header itu, ia akan membaca kelebihan data dari respons permintaan terowongan Anda.',
    "As you're effectively mixing the [[C0]] header from one response with the body of another, using this technique successfully is a bit of a balancing act." => 'Karena Anda mencampurkan [[C0]] satu respons dengan body respons lain, ukuran panjangnya harus diseimbangkan dengan tepat.',
    "If the endpoint to which you send your [[C0]] request returns a resource that is shorter than the tunnelled response you're trying to read, it may be truncated before you can see anything interesting, as in the example above. On the other hand, if the returned [[C1]] is longer than the response to your tunnelled request, you will likely encounter a timeout as the front-end server is left waiting for additional bytes to arrive from the back-end." => 'Jika endpoint [[C0]] terlalu pendek, respons terowongan terpotong; jika [[C1]] terlalu panjang, front-end akan timeout menunggu sisa byte.',
    'Fortunately, with a bit of trial and error, you can often overcome these issues using one of the following solutions:' => 'Kendala panjang ini dapat diatasi dengan salah satu cara berikut:',
    'Point your [[C0]] request to a different endpoint that returns a longer or shorter resource as required.' => 'Arahkan permintaan [[C0]] ke endpoint lain dengan ukuran konten yang sesuai.',
    "If the resource is too short, use a reflected input in the main [[C0]] request to inject arbitrary padding characters. Even though you won't actually see your input being reflected, the returned [[C1]] will still increase accordingly." => 'Jika terlalu pendek, manfaatkan parameter terefleksi di permintaan [[C0]] untuk menambah panjang [[C1]] respons.',
    'If the resource is too long, use a reflected input in the tunnelled request to inject arbitrary characters so that the length of the tunnelled response matches or exceeds the length of the expected content.' => 'Jika terlalu panjang, tambahkan karakter pengisi ke respons permintaan terowongan lewat parameter terefleksi agar mencukupi panjang yang diharapkan.',
    'Web cache poisoning via HTTP/2 request tunnelling' => 'Web cache poisoning melalui HTTP/2 request tunnelling',
    "Even though request tunnelling is generally more limited than classic request smuggling, you can sometimes still construct high-severity attacks. For example, you may be able to combine the request tunnelling techniques we've looked at so far for an extra-powerful form of web cache poisoning." => 'Teknik request tunnelling non-blind dapat dipadukan dengan caching front-end untuk melakukan web cache poisoning tingkat lanjut.',
    'With non-blind request tunnelling, you can effectively mix and match the headers from one response with the body of another. If the response in the body reflects unencoded user input, you may be able to leverage this behavior for reflected XSS in contexts where the browser would not normally execute the code.' => 'Anda bisa menggabungkan header satu respons dengan body respons lain sehingga respons JSON berisi input tak tersanitasi disajikan sebagai HTML dan memicu XSS.',
    'For example, the following response contains unencoded, attacker-controllable input:' => 'Misalnya, respons berikut berisi input tanpa encoding di dalam JSON:',
    "By itself, this is relatively harmless. The [[C0]] means that this payload will simply be interpreted as JSON by the browser. But consider what would happen if you tunnel the request to the back-end instead. This response would appear inside the body of a different response, effectively inheriting its headers, including the [[C1]]." => 'Jika berdiri sendiri, [[C0]] dianggap JSON biasa oleh browser. Namun lewat tunnelling ke dalam respons ber-header [[C1]], skripnya ikut tereksekusi.',
    'As caching takes place on the front-end, caches can also be tricked into serving these mixed responses to other users.' => 'Karena cache beroperasi di front-end, respons campuran berbahaya ini ikut tersimpan dan disajikan ke pengguna lain.',

    // response-queue-poisoning
    "Response queue poisoning is a powerful form of request smuggling attack that causes a front-end server to start mapping responses from the back-end to the wrong requests. In practice, this means that all users of the same front-end/back-end connection are persistently served responses that were intended for someone else." => 'Response queue poisoning menyebabkan front-end memasangkan respons back-end ke permintaan yang salah, sehingga pengguna menerima balasan milik orang lain.',
    'This is achieved by smuggling a complete request, thereby eliciting two responses from the back-end when the front-end server is only expecting one.' => 'Hal ini dicapai dengan menyelundupkan satu permintaan penuh sehingga back-end mengirim dua respons padahal front-end hanya mengharapkan satu.',
    'What is the impact of response queue poisoning?' => 'Apa dampak response queue poisoning?',
    "The impact of response queue poisoning is normally catastrophic. Once the queue is poisoned, an attacker can capture other users' responses simply by issuing arbitrary follow-up requests. These responses may contain sensitive personal or business data, as well as session tokens and the like, which effectively grant the attacker full access to the victim's account." => 'Dampaknya sangat fatal: penyerang cukup mengirim permintaan acak untuk mengambil respons pengguna lain yang mungkin berisi token sesi dan data pribadi.',
    'Response queue poisoning also causes significant collateral damage, effectively breaking the site for any other users whose traffic is being sent to the back-end over the same TCP connection. While attempting to browse the site as normal, users will receive seemingly random responses from the server, which will prevent most functions from working correctly.' => 'Serangan ini juga merusak pengalaman seluruh pengguna lain pada koneksi TCP yang sama karena mereka menerima respons acak.',
    'How to construct a response queue poisoning attack' => 'Cara menyusun serangan response queue poisoning',
    'For a successful response queue poisoning attack, the following criteria must be met:' => 'Agar response queue poisoning berhasil, tiga syarat berikut harus terpenuhi:',
    'The TCP connection between the front-end server and back-end server is [[A0]]reused for multiple request/response cycles[[/A0]].' => 'Koneksi TCP antara front-end dan back-end [[A0]]dipakai ulang untuk banyak siklus permintaan-respons[[/A0]].',
    'The attacker is able to successfully smuggle a complete, standalone request that receives its own distinct response from the back-end server.' => 'Penyerang dapat menyelundupkan satu permintaan penuh yang menghasilkan respons tersendiri dari back-end.',
    "The attack does not result in either server closing the TCP connection. Servers generally close incoming connections when they receive an invalid request because they can't determine where the request is supposed to end." => 'Serangan tidak boleh memicu penutupan koneksi TCP oleh server akibat permintaan cacat.',
    'Understanding the aftermath of request smuggling' => 'Memahami sisa byte pasca request smuggling',
    "Request smuggling attacks usually involve smuggling a partial request, which the server adds as a prefix to the start of the next request on the connection. It's important to note that the content of the smuggled request influences what happens to the connection following the initial attack." => 'Request smuggling biasa menyelundupkan potongan permintaan awal (prefix) yang memengaruhi nasib koneksi sesudahnya bergantung struktur body yang dikirim.',
    'If you just smuggle a request line with some headers, assuming that another request is sent on the connection shortly afterwards, the back-end ultimately still sees two complete requests.' => 'Jika hanya menyelundupkan baris permintaan dan header, back-end tetap melihat dua permintaan lengkap yang rapi.',
    'If you instead smuggle a request that also contains a body, the next request on the connection will be appended to the body of the smuggled request. This often has the side-effect of truncating the final request based on the apparent [[C0]]. As a result, the back-end effectively sees three requests, where the third "request" is just a series of leftover bytes:' => 'Sebaliknya jika menyertakan body, sebagian permintaan berikutnya terpotong oleh [[C0]] sehingga menyisakan potongan byte mentah:',
    'As these leftover bytes don\'t form a valid request, this typically results in an error, causing the server to close the connection.' => 'Karena sisa byte tersebut bukan permintaan HTTP yang sah, server memunculkan error dan langsung menutup koneksi.',
    'Smuggling a complete request' => 'Menyelundupkan permintaan utuh',
    'With a bit of care, you can smuggle a complete request instead of just a prefix. As long as you send exactly two requests in one, any subsequent requests on the connection will remain unchanged:' => 'Dengan menyusun dua permintaan penuh secara tepat tanpa sisa byte, permintaan berikutnya di koneksi tetap utuh:',
    'Notice that no invalid requests are hitting the back-end, so the connection should remain open following the attack.' => 'Karena tidak ada permintaan cacat, koneksi TCP tetap terbuka pasca serangan.',
    'Desynchronizing the response queue' => 'Mendesinkronkan antrean respons',
    'When you smuggle a complete request, the front-end server still thinks it only forwarded a single request. On the other hand, the back-end sees two distinct requests, and will send two responses accordingly:' => 'Front-end mengira hanya mengirim satu permintaan sementara back-end membalas dengan dua respons:',
    'The front-end correctly maps the first response to the initial "wrapper" request and forwards this on to the client. As there are no further requests awaiting a response, the unexpected second response is held in a queue on the connection between the front-end and back-end.' => 'Front-end mengembalikan respons pertama ke penyerang dan menahan respons kedua di antrean koneksi.',
    'When the front-end receives another request, it forwards this to the back-end as normal. However, when issuing the response, it will send the first one in the queue, that is, the leftover response to the smuggled request.' => 'Saat permintaan pengguna berikutnya datang, front-end memberikan respons sisa yang antre lebih dulu ketimbang jawaban aslinya.',
    'The correct response from the back-end is then left without a matching request. This cycle is repeated every time a new request is forwarded down the same connection to the back-end.' => 'Akibatnya respons asli pengguna tersebut tertinggal di antrean dan bergeser satu langkah untuk semua pengguna berikutnya.',
    "Stealing other users' responses" => 'Mencuri respons pengguna lain',
    "Once the response queue is poisoned, the attacker can just send an arbitrary request to capture another user's response." => 'Setelah antrean teracuni, penyerang cukup mengirim permintaan sembarang untuk mengambil respons milik pengguna sebelumnya.',
    'They have no control over which responses they receive as they will always be sent the next response in the queue i.e. the response to the previous user\'s request. In some cases, this will be of limited interest. However, using tools like Burp Intruder, an attacker can easily automate the process of reissuing the request. By doing so, they can quickly grab an assortment of responses intended for different users, at least some of which are likely to contain useful data.' => 'Penyerang akan menerima respons berikutnya secara acak dari antrean. Dengan mengirim permintaan berulang secara otomatis, berbagai respons pengguna berisi data berharga cepat terkumpul.',
    "An attacker can continue to steal responses like this for as long as the front-end/back-end connection remains open. Exactly when a connection is closed differs from server to server, but a common default is to terminate a connection after it has handled 100 requests. It's also trivial to repoison a new connection once the current one is closed." => 'Pencurian terus berjalan selama koneksi belum ditutup (umumnya tiap sekitar 100 permintaan) dan mudah diracuni ulang bila terbuka koneksi baru.',
    'To make it easier to differentiate stolen responses from responses to your own requests, try using a non-existent path in both of the requests that you send. That way, your own requests should consistently receive a 404 response, for example.' => 'Gunakan path fiktif (404) pada permintaan Anda agar respons curian milik pengguna lain (misalnya 200 atau 302) mudah dikenali.',
    'This attack is possible both via classic HTTP/1 request smuggling and by exploiting HTTP/2 downgrading.' => 'Serangan ini dapat dilakukan baik lewat HTTP/1 klasik maupun HTTP/2 downgrading.',

    // browser
    "In this section, you'll learn how you can craft high-severity exploits without relying on malformed requests that browsers will never send. Not only does this expose a whole new range of websites to server-side request smuggling, it enables you to perform client-side variations of these attacks by inducing a victim's browser to poison its own connection to a vulnerable web server." => 'Pada bagian ini, Anda mempelajari cara menyusun eksploitasi berdampak tinggi memakai permintaan valid standar browser, membuka celah request smuggling sisi server maupun serangan sisi klien.',
    'The materials and labs in this section are based on [[A0]]Browser-Powered Desync Attacks: A New Frontier in HTTP Request Smuggling[[/A0]] by SecDevLab Research. This research also led to the discovery of a bypass technique for Host header filters, using [[A1]]connection-state flaws[[/A1]].' => 'Materi dan lab pada bagian ini merujuk pada riset [[A0]]Browser-Powered Desync Attacks[[/A0]], termasuk temuan bypass filter header Host melalui [[A1]]kelemahan status koneksi[[/A1]].',
    'CL.0 request smuggling' => 'CL.0 request smuggling',
    "Back-end servers can sometimes be persuaded to ignore the [[C0]] header, which effectively means they ignore the body of incoming requests. This paves the way for request smuggling attacks that don't rely on [[A0]]chunked transfer encoding[[/A0]] or [[A1]]HTTP/2 downgrading[[/A1]]." => 'Server back-end terkadang mengabaikan header [[C0]] dan tidak membaca body permintaan, membuka jalan request smuggling tanpa [[A0]]chunked encoding[[/A0]] atau [[A1]]downgrading HTTP/2[[/A1]].',
    '[[A0]][[S0]]LEARN[[/S0]] CL.0 request smuggling[[/A0]]' => '[[A0]][[S0]]MATERI[[/S0]] Request smuggling CL.0[[/A0]]',
    '[[A0]][[S0]]LAB[[/S0]] CL.0 request smuggling[[/A0]]' => '[[A0]][[S0]]LAB PRAKTIK[[/S0]] Request smuggling CL.0[[/A0]]',
    'Client-side desync attacks' => 'Serangan client-side desync',
    "Request smuggling is traditionally considered a server-side issue because it can only be exploited using specialist tools like Burp Repeater - standard browsers simply won't send the kinds of requests needed to trigger the desync. However, building on the lessons learned from [[A0]]CL.0 attacks[[/A0]], it's sometimes possible to cause a desync using fully browser-compatible HTTP/1 requests." => 'Meski selama ini dianggap masalah server-side, berbekal pola [[A0]]serangan CL.0[[/A0]], desinkronisasi kini dapat dipicu menggunakan permintaan HTTP/1 standar dari browser.',
    "You can use these browser-compatible requests to trigger a client-side desync (CSD) between a browser and vulnerable web server, enabling attacks on single-server sites, which are otherwise immune to request smuggling, and intranet sites that you can't access directly." => 'Permintaan tersebut memungkinkan terjadinya client-side desync (CSD) antara browser dan server web rentan, termasuk pada situs server tunggal maupun intranet.',
    '[[A0]][[S0]]LEARN[[/S0]] Client-side desync attacks[[/A0]]' => '[[A0]][[S0]]MATERI[[/S0]] Serangan client-side desync[[/A0]]',
    '[[A0]][[S0]]LAB[[/S0]] Client-side desync[[/A0]]' => '[[A0]][[S0]]LAB PRAKTIK[[/S0]] Client-side desync[[/A0]]',
    'Seemingly secure websites may contain hidden desync vulnerabilities that only reveal themselves if you pause mid-request. By sending the headers, promising a body, and then just waiting, you can sometimes find additional desync vulnerabilities that can be used for both server-side and client-side exploits.' => 'Situs yang tampak aman bisa menyimpan celah pause-based desync jika pengirim berhenti sejenak di tengah permintaan setelah mengirim header sebelum body.',
    '[[A0]][[S0]]LEARN[[/S0]] Pause-based desync attacks[[/A0]]' => '[[A0]][[S0]]MATERI[[/S0]] Serangan pause-based desync[[/A0]]',
    '[[A0]][[S0]]LAB[[/S0]] Server-side pause-based request smuggling[[/A0]]' => '[[A0]][[S0]]LAB PRAKTIK[[/S0]] Pause-based request smuggling sisi server[[/A0]]',

    // cl-0
    "Request smuggling vulnerabilities are the result of discrepancies in how chained systems determine where each request starts and ends. This is typically due to [[A0]]inconsistent header parsing[[/A0]], leading to one server using a request's [[C0]] and the other treating the message as chunked. However, it's possible to perform many of the same attacks without relying on either of these issues." => 'Selain akibat [[A0]]ketidakkonsistenan parsing header[[/A0]] dengan [[C0]] dan chunked encoding, request smuggling juga dapat terjadi ketika back-end mengabaikan panjang body sama sekali.',
    'In some instances, servers can be persuaded to ignore the [[C0]] header, meaning they assume that each request finishes at the end of the headers. This is effectively the same as treating the [[C1]] as [[C2]].' => 'Pada kondisi tertentu, server mengabaikan header [[C0]] dan menganggap permintaan selesai tepat di akhir header (setara [[C1]] bernilai [[C2]]).',
    'If the back-end server exhibits this behavior, but the front-end still uses the [[C0]] header to determine where the request ends, you can potentially exploit this discrepancy for HTTP request smuggling. We\'ve decided to call this a "CL.0" vulnerability.' => 'Jika back-end bersikap demikian sementara front-end tetap membaca header [[C0]], selisih inilah yang disebut kerentanan "CL.0".',
    'The materials and labs in this section are based on [[A0]]Browser-Powered Desync Attacks: A New Frontier in HTTP Request Smuggling[[/A0]] by SecDevLab Research.' => 'Materi dan lab pada bagian ini merujuk pada publikasi riset [[A0]]Browser-Powered Desync Attacks: A New Frontier in HTTP Request Smuggling[[/A0]].',
    'Testing for CL.0 vulnerabilities' => 'Menguji kerentanan CL.0',
    'To probe for CL.0 vulnerabilities, first send a request containing another partial request in its body, then send a normal follow-up request. You can then check to see whether the response to the follow-up request was affected by the smuggled prefix.' => 'Untuk menguji celah CL.0, kirim permintaan berisi potongan permintaan di dalam body lalu ikuti permintaan normal di koneksi yang sama dan periksa apakah responsnya terpengaruh.',
    'In the following example, the follow-up request for the home page has received a 404 response. This strongly suggests that the back-end server interpreted the body of the [[C0]] request ([[C1]]) as the start of another request.' => 'Pada contoh berikut, permintaan normal ke beranda justru menerima status 404, menandakan back-end mengira body permintaan [[C0]] ([[C1]]) sebagai awal permintaan baru.',
    "Crucially, notice that we haven't tampered with the headers in any way - the length of the request is specified by a perfectly normal, accurate [[C0]] header." => 'Perhatikan bahwa header sama sekali tidak dimanipulasi dan [[C0]] benar-benar sesuai ukuran body.',
    'To try this yourself using Burp Repeater:' => 'Langkah pengujian berurutan lewat satu koneksi:',
    'Create one tab containing the setup request and another containing an arbitrary follow-up request.' => 'Siapkan permintaan pemicu pertama dan permintaan lanjutan kedua secara berurutan.',
    'Add the two tabs to a group in the correct order.' => 'Kelompokkan kedua permintaan sesuai urutan.',
    'Using the drop-down menu next to the <strong>Send</strong> button, change the send mode to <strong>Send group in sequence (single connection)</strong>.' => 'Atur mode pengiriman agar mengirim keduanya berurutan melalui satu koneksi TCP tunggal.',
    'Change the [[C0]] header to [[C1]].' => 'Pastikan header [[C0]] bernilai [[C1]].',
    'Send the sequence and check the responses.' => 'Kirim rangkaian permintaan lalu amati responsnya.',
    "In the wild, we've mostly observed this behavior on endpoints that simply aren't expecting [[C0]] requests, so they implicitly assume that no requests have a body. Endpoints that trigger server-level redirects and requests for static files are prime candidates." => 'Perilaku CL.0 paling sering ditemukan di endpoint yang tidak mengantisipasi permintaan [[C0]] seperti aset statis atau pemicu redirect.',
    'Eliciting CL.0 behavior' => 'Memicu perilaku CL.0 secara paksa',
    "If you can't find any endpoints that appear vulnerable, you can try eliciting this behavior instead." => 'Jika tidak menemukan endpoint rentan secara langsung, coba paksa server memunculkan perilaku CL.0.',
    "When a request's headers trigger a server error, some servers issue an error response without consuming the request body off the socket. If they don't close the connection afterwards, this can provide an alternative CL.0 desync vector." => 'Sebagian server membalas pesan error akibat header cacat tanpa membaca sisa body di soket dan tetap membiarkan koneksi terbuka.',
    "You can also try using [[C0]] requests with an obfuscated [[C1]] header. If you're able to hide this from the back-end server but not the front-end, this also has the potential to cause a desync. We looked at some [[A0]]header obfuscation techniques[[/A0]] when we covered TE.TE request smuggling." => 'Coba juga permintaan [[C0]] dengan header [[C1]] yang disamarkan lewat [[A0]]teknik penyamaran header[[/A0]] agar hanya terbaca oleh front-end.',
    'Exploiting CL.0 vulnerabilities' => 'Mengeksploitasi kerentanan CL.0',
    "You can exploit CL.0 vulnerabilities for the same server-side request smuggling attacks we've covered in our [[A0]]previous request smuggling materials[[/A0]]. Try this for yourself with the following lab." => 'Kerentanan CL.0 dapat dimanfaatkan untuk seluruh skenario serangan di [[A0]]materi eksploitasi request smuggling sebelumnya[[/A0]].',
    'H2.0 vulnerabilities' => 'Kerentanan H2.0',
    'Websites that [[A0]]downgrade HTTP/2 requests to HTTP/1[[/A0]] may be vulnerable to an equivalent "H2.0" issue if the back-end server ignores the [[C0]] header of the downgraded request.' => 'Situs yang melakukan [[A0]]downgrade HTTP/2 ke HTTP/1[[/A0]] juga dapat terkena varian "H2.0" apabila back-end mengabaikan [[C0]] hasil terjemahan.',
    'How to prevent CL.0 vulnerabilities' => 'Cara mencegah kerentanan CL.0',
    'For some high-level measures you can take to prevent CL.0 vulnerabilities, and other forms of desync attack, see [[A0]]How to prevent HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities[[/A0]].' => 'Untuk langkah pencegahan CL.0 dan desync lainnya, lihat [[A0]]Cara mencegah kerentanan HTTP request smuggling[[/A0]].',
    "You can expand on what you've learned in this section to perform client-side variations of a number of request smuggling attacks. To learn how, check out [[A0]]Client-side desync attacks[[/A0]] [[S0]]LABS[[/S0]]" => 'Anda dapat memperluas konsep ini ke sisi klien pada [[A0]]Serangan client-side desync[[/A0]] [[S0]]LAB PRAKTIK[[/S0]]',

    // client-side-desync
    "Classic desync or request smuggling attacks rely on intentionally malformed requests that ordinary browsers simply won't send. This limits these attacks to websites that use a front-end/back-end architecture. However, as we've learned from looking at [[A0]]CL.0 attacks[[/A0]], it's possible to cause a desync using fully browser-compatible HTTP/1.1 requests. Not only does this open up new possibilities for server-side request smuggling, it enables a whole new class of threat - client-side desync attacks." => 'Serangan desync klasik mengandalkan permintaan cacat yang tidak dikirim browser sehingga hanya berdampak pada arsitektur berlapis. Berkat pola [[A0]]serangan CL.0[[/A0]], permintaan HTTP/1.1 sah dari browser pun dapat memicu serangan baru bernama client-side desync.',
    'What is a client-side desync attack?' => 'Apa itu serangan client-side desync?',
    "A client-side desync (CSD) is an attack that makes the victim's web browser desynchronize its own connection to the vulnerable website. This can be contrasted with regular request smuggling attacks, which desynchronize the connection between a front-end and back-end server." => 'Client-side desync (CSD) memaksa browser korban mendesinkronkan koneksinya sendiri terhadap situs target yang rentan, berbeda dari request smuggling tradisional yang mendesinkronkan front-end dan back-end.',
    'Web servers can sometimes be encouraged to respond to [[C0]] requests without reading in the body. If they subsequently allow the browser to reuse the same connection for additional requests, this results in a client-side desync vulnerability.' => 'Ketika server merespons permintaan [[C0]] tanpa membaca body lalu membiarkan browser memakai ulang koneksi tersebut, timbul celah client-side desync.',
    'In high-level terms, a CSD attack involves the following stages:' => 'Secara garis besar, serangan CSD berjalan dalam empat tahap:',
    'The victim visits a web page on an arbitrary domain containing malicious JavaScript.' => 'Korban mengunjungi halaman web sembarang yang memuat JavaScript berbahaya.',
    "The JavaScript causes the victim's browser to issue a request to the vulnerable website. This contains an attacker-controlled request prefix in its body, much like a normal request smuggling attack." => 'Skrip memicu browser mengirim permintaan ke situs target dengan prefix permintaan selundupan di dalam body.',
    "The malicious prefix is left on the server's TCP/TLS socket after it responds to the initial request, desyncing the connection with the browser." => 'Setelah server merespons, sisa prefix tertinggal di soket TCP/TLS dan mendesinkronkan koneksi browser.',
    'The JavaScript then triggers a follow-up request down the poisoned connection. This is appended to the malicious prefix, eliciting a harmful response from the server.' => 'Skrip lalu mengirim permintaan kedua melalui koneksi teracuni tersebut hingga menghasilkan respons berbahaya.',
    "As these attacks don't rely on parsing discrepancies between two servers, this means that even single-server websites may be vulnerable." => 'Karena tidak butuh dua server terpisah, situs server tunggal sekalipun tetap dapat rentan terhadap CSD.',
    "For these attacks to work, it's important to note that the target web server must not support HTTP/2. Client-side desyncs rely on HTTP/1.1 connection reuse, and browsers generally favor HTTP/2 where available." => 'Agar bekerja, server target umumnya tidak memakai HTTP/2 karena CSD mengandalkan koneksi ulang HTTP/1.1.',
    'One exception to this rule is if you suspect that your intended victim will access the site via a forward proxy that only supports HTTP/1.1.' => 'Pengecualian berlaku jika korban mengakses situs melalui forward proxy yang hanya mendukung HTTP/1.1.',
    'Testing for client-side desync vulnerabilities' => 'Menguji kerentanan client-side desync',
    "Due to the added complexity of relying on a browser to deliver your attack, it's important to be methodical when testing for client-side desync vulnerabilities. Although it may be tempting to jump ahead at times, we recommend the following workflow. This ensures that you confirm your assumptions about each element of the attack in stages." => 'Karena melibatkan perilaku browser, uji celah client-side desync secara bertahap agar tiap asumsi terbukti sebelum dirangkai:',
    '[[A0]]Probe for potential desync vectors in Burp.[[/A0]]' => '[[A0]]Cari kandidat vektor desync terlebih dahulu.[[/A0]]',
    '[[A0]]Confirm the desync vector in Burp.[[/A0]]' => '[[A0]]Konfirmasi vektor desync pada koneksi tunggal.[[/A0]]',
    '[[A0]]Build a proof of concept to replicate the behavior in a browser.[[/A0]]' => '[[A0]]Buat bukti konsep (PoC) di browser nyata.[[/A0]]',
    'Identify an exploitable gadget.' => 'Temukan gadget yang dapat dieksploitasi.',
    'Construct a working [[A0]]exploit[[/A0]] in Burp.' => 'Susun [[A0]]eksploitasi[[/A0]] penuh.',
    'Replicate the [[A0]]exploit[[/A0]] in your browser.' => 'Replikasi [[A0]]eksploitasi[[/A0]] langsung di browser.',
    "Both Burp Scanner and the [[A0]]HTTP Request Smuggler[[/A0]] extension can help you automate much of this process, but it's useful to know how to do this manually to cement your understanding of how it works." => 'Ekstensi seperti [[A0]]HTTP Request Smuggler[[/A0]] membantu otomasi, namun penguasaan langkah manual penting untuk troubleshooting.',
    'Probing for client-side desync vectors' => 'Mencari vektor client-side desync',
    'The first step in testing for client-side desync vulnerabilities is to identify or craft a request that causes the server to ignore the [[C0]] header. The simplest way to probe for this behavior is by sending a request in which the specified [[C1]] is longer than the actual body:' => 'Langkah awal adalah mencari permintaan yang membuat server mengabaikan [[C0]] dengan mengirim body lebih pendek daripada nilai [[C1]]:',
    'If the request just hangs or times out, this suggests that the server is waiting for the remaining bytes promised by the headers.' => 'Jika permintaan menggantung hingga timeout, server masih menunggu sisa body sesuai header.',
    "If you get an immediate response, you've potentially found a CSD vector. This warrants further investigation." => 'Jika server langsung membalas seketika, Anda berpotensi menemukan vektor CSD.',
    "As with [[A0]]CL.0 vulnerabilities[[/A0]], we've found that the most likely candidates are endpoints that aren't expecting [[C0]] requests, such as static files or server-level redirects." => 'Seperti pada [[A0]]celah CL.0[[/A0]], kandidat terbaik adalah endpoint file statis dan pengalihan yang tidak mengharapkan metode [[C0]].',
    'Alternatively, you may be able to elicit this behavior by triggering a server error. In this case, remember that you still need a request that a browser will send cross-domain. In practice, this means you can only tamper with the URL, body, plus a few odds and ends like the [[C0]] header and latter part of the [[C1]] header.' => 'Atau picu error server memakai bagian yang diizinkan lintas domain di browser, seperti URL, body, [[C0]], atau akhiran [[C1]].',
    "You may also be able to trigger server errors by attempting to navigate above the web root. Just remember that browsers normalize the path, so you'll need to URL encode the characters for your traversal sequence:" => 'Traversal di atas web root juga dapat memicu error server, cukup URL-encode karakternya agar tidak dinormalisasi browser:',
    'Confirming the desync vector in Burp' => 'Mengonfirmasi vektor desync',
    "It's important to note that some secure servers respond without waiting for the body but still parse it correctly when it arrives. Other servers don't handle the [[C0]] correctly but close the connection immediately after responding, making them unexploitable." => 'Sebagian server tetap membaca body di belakang layar atau menutup koneksi tepat setelah merespons meski mengabaikan [[C0]], sehingga tidak bisa dieksploitasi.',
    'To filter these out, try sending two requests down the same connection to see if you can use the body of the first request to affect the response to the second one, just like you would when [[A0]]probing for CL.0 request smuggling[[/A0]].' => 'Pastikan celahnya nyata dengan mengirim dua permintaan lewat satu koneksi persisten seperti langkah [[A0]]pengujian CL.0[[/A0]].',
    'Building a proof of concept in a browser' => 'Membangun PoC di dalam browser',
    "Once you've identified a suitable vector using Burp, it's important to confirm that you can replicate the desync in a browser." => 'Setelah vektor teruji, pastikan desinkronisasi yang sama berhasil direplikasi dari dalam browser.',
    'To reduce the chance of any interference and ensure that your test simulates an arbitrary victim\'s browser as closely as possible:' => 'Untuk meminimalkan gangguan selama pengujian browser:',
    'Use a browser that is <strong>not</strong> proxying traffic through Burp Suite - using any HTTP proxy can have a significant impact on the success of your attacks. We recommend Chrome as its developer tools provide some useful troubleshooting features.' => 'Gunakan browser <strong>tanpa</strong> proxy HTTP agar pengelolaan kolam koneksi TCP tetap akurat seperti browser korban.',
    'Disable any browser extensions.' => 'Nonaktifkan sementara ekstensi browser yang dapat mengganggu permintaan.',
    'Go to the site from which you plan to launch the attack on the victim. This must be on a different domain to the vulnerable site and be accessed over HTTPS. For the purpose of our labs, you can use the provided exploit server.' => 'Buka situs penyerang (atau exploit server lab) di domain lain melalui HTTPS.',
    'Open the browser\'s developer tools and go to the <strong>Network</strong> tab.' => 'Buka Developer Tools browser lalu pilih tab <strong>Network</strong>.',
    'Make the following adjustments:' => 'Sesuaikan pengaturan berikut:',
    'Select the <strong>Preserve log</strong> option.' => 'Centang opsi <strong>Preserve log</strong>.',
    'Right-click on the headers and enable the <strong>Connection ID</strong> column.' => 'Klik kanan kepala kolom dan tampilkan kolom <strong>Connection ID</strong>.',
    'This ensures that each request that the browser sends is logged on the <strong>Network</strong> tab, along with details of which connection it used. This can help with troubleshooting any issues later.' => 'Pengaturan ini memastikan seluruh riwayat permintaan beserta ID koneksinya tetap tercatat di tab <strong>Network</strong>.',
    'Switch to the <strong>Console</strong> tab and use [[C0]] to replicate the desync probe you tested in Burp. The code should look something like this:' => 'Buka tab <strong>Console</strong> dan jalankan [[C0]] seperti contoh berikut untuk menguji ulang desync:',
    'In addition to specifying the [[C0]] method and adding our malicious prefix to the body, notice that we\'ve set the following options:' => 'Selain metode [[C0]] dan prefix berbahaya di body, perhatikan opsi berikut:',
    '[[C0]] - This ensures that the connection ID of each request is visible on the <strong>Network</strong> tab, which can help with troubleshooting.' => '[[C0]] agar ID koneksi tetap terlihat jelas di tab <strong>Network</strong>.',
    '[[C0]] - Browsers generally use separate connection pools for requests with cookies and those without. This option ensures that you\'re poisoning the "with-cookies" pool, which you\'ll want for most exploits.' => '[[C0]] karena browser memisahkan connection pool berkuki dan tanpa kuki, sedangkan eksploitasi menyasar sesi berkuki.',
    'When you run this command, you should see two requests on the <strong>Network</strong> tab. The first request should receive the usual response. If the second request receives the response to the malicious prefix (in this case, a 404), this confirms that you have successfully triggered a desync from your browser.' => 'Jika permintaan kedua memperoleh balasan milik prefix selundupan (misalnya 404), client-side desync di browser terkonfirmasi berhasil.',
    'Handling redirects' => 'Menangani pengalihan (redirect)',
    "As we've mentioned already, requests to endpoints that trigger server-level redirects are a common vector for client-side desyncs. When building an exploit, this presents a minor obstacle because browsers will follow this redirect, breaking the attack sequence. Thankfully, there's an easy workaround." => 'Jika vektor CSD memicu redirect, browser otomatis mengikutinya dan merusak alur serangan. Hal ini mudah diakali lewat mode CORS.',
    'By setting the [[C0]] option for the initial request, you can intentionally trigger a CORS error, which prevents the browser from following the redirect. You can then resume the attack sequence by invoking [[C1]] instead of [[C2]]. For example:' => 'Setel [[C0]] pada permintaan pertama agar memicu galat CORS sehingga redirect dibatalkan, lalu lanjutkan serangan di dalam [[C1]] menggantikan [[C2]]:',
    'The downside to this approach is that you won\'t be able to see the connection ID on the <strong>Network</strong> tab, which may make troubleshooting more difficult.' => 'Kekurangannya, ID koneksi tidak lagi tampil pada tab <strong>Network</strong> saat mode ini dipakai.',
    'Exploiting client-side desync vulnerabilities' => 'Mengeksploitasi kerentanan client-side desync',
    "Once you've [[A0]]found a suitable vector[[/A0]] and [[A1]]confirmed that you can successfully cause the desync in a browser[[/A1]], you're ready to start looking for exploitable gadgets." => 'Begitu [[A0]]vektor ditemukan[[/A0]] dan [[A1]]terbukti di browser[[/A1]], saatnya mencari gadget eksploitasi.',
    'Client-side variations of classic attacks' => 'Variasi sisi klien dari serangan klasik',
    'You can use these techniques to perform many of the [[A0]]same attacks[[/A0]] as you can with server-side request smuggling. All you need is for the victim to visit a malicious website that causes their browser to launch the attack.' => 'Berbagai [[A0]]skenario serangan klasik[[/A0]] dapat dijalankan di sisi klien cukup dengan memancing korban membuka halaman penyerang.',
    'Client-side cache poisoning' => 'Client-side cache poisoning',
    "We previously covered how you can use a server-side desync to [[A0]]turn an on-site redirect into an open redirect[[/A0]], enabling you to hijack a JavaScript resource import. You can achieve the same effect just using a client-side desync, but it can be tricky to poison the right connection at the right time. It's much easier to use a desync to poison the browser's cache instead. This way, you don't need to worry about which connection it uses to load the resource." => 'Selain membajak koneksi secara langsung lewat [[A0]]open redirect[[/A0]], client-side desync jauh lebih praktis dipakai untuk meracuni cache lokal browser korban.',
    "In this section, we'll walk you through the process of constructing this attack. This involves the following high-level steps:" => 'Tahapan penyusunan serangan client-side cache poisoning adalah sebagai berikut:',
    '[[A0]]Identify a suitable CSD vector[[/A0]] and desync the browser\'s connection.' => '[[A0]]Temukan vektor CSD[[/A0]] dan desinkronkan koneksi browser.',
    '[[A0]]Use the desynced connection to poison the cache with a redirect.[[/A0]]' => '[[A0]]Gunakan koneksi tersebut untuk meracuni cache dengan redirect.[[/A0]]',
    '[[A0]]Trigger the resource import from the target domain.[[/A0]]' => '[[A0]]Picu pemuatan skrip dari domain target.[[/A0]]',
    '[[A0]]Deliver a payload.[[/A0]]' => '[[A0]]Kirim payload eksekusi JavaScript.[[/A0]]',
    'When testing this attack in a browser, make sure you clear your cache between each attempt (<strong>Settings &gt; Clear browsing data &gt; Cached images and files</strong>).' => 'Selama pengujian di browser, selalu bersihkan cache di antara tiap percobaan.',
    'Poisoning the cache with a redirect' => 'Meracuni cache dengan redirect',
    "Once you've [[A0]]found a CSD vector[[/A0]] and [[A1]]confirmed that you can replicate it in a browser[[/A1]], you need to identify a suitable [[A2]]redirect gadget[[/A2]]. After that, poisoning the cache is fairly straightforward." => 'Setelah [[A0]]vektor CSD[[/A0]] teruji dan [[A1]]berjalan di browser[[/A1]], padukan dengan [[A2]]gadget redirect[[/A2]].',
    'First, tweak your proof of concept so that the smuggled prefix will trigger a redirect to the domain where you\'ll host your malicious payload. Next, change the follow-up request to a direct request for the target JavaScript file.' => 'Ubah prefix agar mengalihkan ke domain penyerang, lalu arahkan permintaan kedua langsung ke file JavaScript yang diimpor halaman target.',
    'The resulting code should look something like this:' => 'Contoh kodenya menjadi seperti ini:',
    'This will poison the cache, albeit with an infinite redirect back to your script. You can confirm this by viewing the script in a browser and studying the <strong>Network</strong> tab in the developer tools.' => 'Skrip ini meracuni cache target dengan redirect menuju halaman Anda, yang dapat diamati pada tab <strong>Network</strong>.',
    'You need to trigger the follow-up request via a top-level navigation to the target domain. Due to the way browsers partition their cache, issuing a cross-domain request using [[C0]] will poison the wrong cache.' => 'Navigasi permintaan kedua wajib berupa navigasi top-level karena partisi cache browser memisahkan cache permintaan [[C0]] lintas domain.',
    'Triggering the resource import' => 'Memicu pemuatan aset',
    "Sending your victim into an infinite loop may be mildly irritating, but it's not much of an exploit. You now need to further develop your script so that when the browser returns having already poisoned its cache, it is navigated to a page on the vulnerable site that will trigger the resource import. This is easily achieved using conditional statements to execute different code depending on whether the browser window has viewed your script already." => 'Agar tidak berputar tanpa akhir, tambahkan logika kondisi agar setelah cache teracuni, korban diarahkan ke halaman target yang mengimpor file JavaScript tersebut.',
    'When the browser attempts to import the resource on the target site, it will use its poisoned cache entry and be redirected back to your malicious page for a third time.' => 'Saat memuat file skrip, browser mengambil entri cache teracuni dan kembali dialihkan ke server Anda untuk eksekusi.',
    'Delivering a payload' => 'Mengirimkan payload akhir',
    "At this stage, you've laid the foundations for an attack, but the final challenge is working out how to deliver a potentially harmful payload." => 'Tantangan terakhir adalah melayani HTML saat pertama dikunjungi korban, namun melayani JavaScript murni ketika dipanggil lewat tag script.',
    "Initially, the victim's browser loads your malicious page as HTML and executes the nested JavaScript in the context of your own domain. When it eventually attempts to import the JavaScript resource on the target domain and gets redirected to your malicious page, you'll notice that the script doesn't execute. This is because you're still serving HTML when the browser is expecting JavaScript." => 'Jika masih berupa dokumen HTML, tag script target gagal mengeksekusinya karena kesalahan sintaks.',
    'For an actual exploit, you need a way to serve plain JavaScript from the same endpoint, while ensuring that this only executes at this final stage to avoid interfering with the setup requests.' => 'Solusinya adalah menyusun satu respons endpoint yang bekerja valid sebagai HTML maupun JavaScript.',
    'One possible approach is to create a polyglot payload by wrapping the HTML in JavaScript comments:' => 'Salah satu cara adalah membuat payload polyglot dengan membungkus HTML di dalam komentar JavaScript:',
    'When the browser loads the page as HTML, it will only execute the JavaScript in the [[C0]] tags. When it eventually loads this in a JavaScript context, it will only execute the [[C1]] payload, treating the rest of the content as arbitrary developer comments.' => 'Saat dibuka sebagai HTML, browser menjalankan skrip di dalam tag [[C0]]; saat dimuat sebagai skrip, hanya [[C1]] yang tereksekusi sementara bagian HTML diabaikan sebagai komentar.',
    'For more information about how we found this vulnerability in the wild, check out [[A0]]Browser-Powered Desync Attacks: A New Frontier in HTTP Request Smuggling[[/A0]] by SecDevLab Research.' => 'Detail temuan nyatanya dibahas pada [[A0]]Browser-Powered Desync Attacks[[/A0]].',
    'Pivoting attacks against internal infrastructure' => 'Mengarahkan serangan ke infrastruktur internal',
    'Most server-side desync attacks involve manipulating HTTP headers in a way that is only possible using tools like Burp Repeater. For example, it\'s not possible to make someone\'s browser send a request with a log4shell payload in the [[C0]] header:' => 'Sebagian serangan sisi server tidak bisa memaksa browser mengirim header kustom sembarang seperti [[C0]]:',
    'This means that these attacks are normally limited to websites that you can access directly. However, if the website is vulnerable to client-side desyncs, you may be able to achieve the desired effect by inducing a victim\'s browser to send the following request:' => 'Namun lewat CSD, browser korban dipancing menyelundupkan permintaan penuh beserta header kustom di dalam body POST ke jaringan internalnya:',
    "As all of the requests originate from the victim's browser, this potentially enables you to pivot attacks against any website that they have access to. This includes sites located on trusted intranets or that are hidden behind IP-based restrictions. Some browsers are working on mitigations for these types of attack, but these are likely to only have partial coverage." => 'Karena seluruh lalu lintas berasal dari browser korban sendiri, serangan dapat diarahkan ke situs intranet yang dibatasi berdasarkan IP.',
    'How to prevent client-side desync vulnerabilities' => 'Cara mencegah kerentanan client-side desync',
    'For some high-level measures you can take to prevent client-side desync vulnerabilities, and other forms of desync attack, see [[A0]]How to prevent HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities[[/A0]].' => 'Untuk langkah pencegahan client-side desync, lihat [[A0]]Cara mencegah kerentanan HTTP request smuggling[[/A0]].',
    "You've learned about [[A0]]CL.0[[/A0]] and [[A1]]CSD[[/A1]] attacks, but there's another potential desync vector that can enable both server-side and client-side exploits on websites that may initially appear secure. To learn more, check out [[A2]]Pause-based desync vulnerabilities[[/A2]] [[S0]]LABS[[/S0]]" => 'Lanjutkan ke vektor lanjutan berikutnya: [[A2]]Kerentanan pause-based desync[[/A2]] [[S0]]LAB PRAKTIK[[/S0]] (setelah memahami [[A0]]CL.0[[/A0]] dan [[A1]]CSD[[/A1]]).',

    // pause-based-desync
    'Seemingly secure websites may contain hidden desync vulnerabilities that only reveal themselves if you pause mid-request.' => 'Situs yang tampak aman dapat menyimpan celah desync tersembunyi yang baru muncul apabila pengiriman permintaan dijeda di tengah jalan.',
    "Servers are commonly configured with a read timeout. If they don't receive any more data for a certain amount of time, they treat the request as complete and issue a response, regardless of how many bytes they were told to expect. Pause-based desync vulnerabilities can occur when a server times out a request but leaves the connection open for reuse. Given the right conditions, this behavior can provide an alternative vector for both server-side and client-side desync attacks." => 'Server yang mengalami read timeout sering mengirim respons sebelum menerima seluruh body dan membiarkan koneksi TCP tetap terbuka, menciptakan celah pause-based desync.',
    'Server-side pause-based desync' => 'Pause-based desync sisi server',
    'You can potentially use the pause-based technique to elicit [[A0]]CL.0[[/A0]]-like behavior, allowing you to construct server-side request smuggling exploits for websites that may initially appear secure.' => 'Teknik jeda waktu dapat memicu perilaku mirip [[A0]]CL.0[[/A0]] untuk request smuggling sisi server.',
    'This is dependent on the following conditions:' => 'Teknik ini bergantung pada tiga kondisi berikut:',
    'The front-end server must immediately forward each byte of the request to the back-end rather than waiting until it has received the full request.' => 'Front-end langsung mengalirkan byte ke back-end tanpa menunggu permintaan lengkap diterima dulu.',
    'The front-end server must not (or can be encouraged not to) time out requests before the back-end server.' => 'Front-end tidak boleh memutus timeout lebih cepat daripada back-end.',
    'The back-end server must leave the connection open for reuse following a read timeout.' => 'Back-end membiarkan koneksi tetap terbuka setelah mengalami read timeout.',
    "To demonstrate how this technique works, let's walk through an example. The following is a standard [[A0]]CL.0 request smuggling[[/A0]] probe:" => 'Berikut contoh probe standar [[A0]]CL.0 request smuggling[[/A0]]:',
    'Consider what happens if we send the headers to a vulnerable website, but pause before sending the body.' => 'Perhatikan alur berikut saat kita mengirim header terlebih dahulu lalu berhenti sejenak sebelum mengirim body:',
    'The front-end forwards the headers to the back-end, then continues to wait for the remaining bytes promised by the [[C0]] header.' => 'Front-end meneruskan header ke back-end dan menunggu sisa body sesuai [[C0]].',
    'After a while, the back-end times out and sends a response, even though it has only consumed part of the request. At this point, the front-end may or may not read in this response and forward it to us.' => 'Setelah beberapa saat, back-end mengalami timeout dan mengirim respons dini meski body belum diterima.',
    'We finally send the body, which contains a basic request smuggling prefix in this case.' => 'Kita lalu mengirim body berisi prefix permintaan selundupan.',
    'The front-end server treats this as a continuation of the initial request and forwards this to the back-end down the same connection.' => 'Front-end menganggapnya kelanjutan permintaan pertama dan meneruskannya ke koneksi yang sama.',
    'The back-end server has already responded to the initial request, so assumes that these bytes are the start of another request.' => 'Karena sudah selesai merespons, back-end memperlakukan byte body tersebut sebagai awal permintaan baru.',
    'At this point, we have effectively achieved a CL.0 desync, poisoning the front-end/back-end connection with a request prefix.' => 'Pada titik ini desinkronisasi CL.0 tercapai dan koneksi telah diracuni.',
    "We've found that servers are more likely to be vulnerable when they generate a response themselves rather than passing the request to the application." => 'Perilaku ini paling sering muncul saat web server menangani respons langsung, misalnya redirect tingkat server.',
    'Our research team discovered this vulnerability on the widely used Apache HTTP Server, which exhibited this behavior when performing server-level redirects from [[C0]] to [[C1]]. We reported this issue and it has been fixed in version 2.4.53, so make sure to update if you haven\'t already. For more details, check out [[A0]]Browser-Powered Desync Attacks: A New Frontier in HTTP Request Smuggling[[/A0]] by SecDevLab Research.' => 'Celah serupa sempat ditemukan pada redirect server Apache HTTP Server ([[C0]] ke [[C1]]) sebelum ditambal pada versi 2.4.53. Selengkapnya di [[A0]]Browser-Powered Desync Attacks[[/A0]].',
    'Testing for pause-based CL.0 vulnerabilities' => 'Menguji kerentanan pause-based CL.0',
    "It's possible to test for pause-based CL.0 vulnerabilities using Burp Repeater, but only if the front-end server forwards the back-end's post-timeout response to you the moment it's generated, which isn't always the case. We recommend using the [[A0]]Turbo Intruder[[/A0]] extension as it lets you pause mid-request then resume regardless of whether you've received a response." => 'Pengujian paling praktis memakai [[A0]]ekstensi pengiriman berjeda[[/A0]] yang memungkinkan jeda di tengah koneksi meski front-end belum mengembalikan respons dini.',
    'In Burp Repeater, create a CL.0 request smuggling probe like we used in the example above, then send it to Turbo Intruder.' => 'Siapkan permintaan uji CL.0 dengan jeda pengiriman setelah header.',
    "In Turbo Intruder's Python editor panel, adjust the request engine configuration to set the following options:" => 'Atur konfigurasi mesin pengirim agar memakai satu koneksi tunggal tanpa pipelining:',
    'Queue the request, adding the following arguments to the [[C0]] interface:' => 'Antrekan permintaan dengan argumen jeda di [[C0]]:',
    '[[C0]] - A list of strings after which you want Turbo Intruder to pause.' => '[[C0]] sebagai penanda string tempat pengiriman berhenti sejenak.',
    '[[C0]] - The duration of the pause in milliseconds.' => '[[C0]] sebagai durasi jeda dalam milidetik.',
    'For example, to pause after the headers for 60 seconds, queue the request as follows:' => 'Contoh untuk jeda 60 detik tepat setelah blok header:',
    'Queue an arbitrary follow-up request as normal:' => 'Antrekan permintaan normal lanjutan:',
    'Ensure that you\'re logging all responses to the results table:' => 'Catat seluruh respons pada tabel hasil:',
    "When you first start the attack, you won't see any results in the table. However, after the specified pause duration, you should see two results. If the response to the second request matches what you expected from the smuggled prefix (in this case a 404), this strongly suggests that the desync was successful." => 'Setelah durasi jeda berakhir, kedua respons muncul; jika permintaan kedua membalas sesuai prefix selundupan (misalnya 404), desync berhasil.',
    'Instead of using [[C0]] to specify a pause based on string matching, you can use the [[C1]] argument to specify an offset. For example, you could pause before the body by specifying an offset that\'s the inverse of the [[C2]] ([[C3]]).' => 'Selain [[C0]] berbasis pencocokan teks, argumen [[C1]] berbasis offset negatif terhadap [[C2]] ([[C3]]) juga dapat dipakai.',
    'Client-side pause-based desync' => 'Pause-based desync sisi klien',
    "In theory, it may be possible to perform a client-side variation of the pause-based CL.0 desync. Unfortunately, we haven't yet found a reliable way to make a browser pause mid-request. However, there is one possible workaround - an active MITM attack." => 'Variasi sisi klien sulit dipicu langsung dari API browser karena tidak ada kontrol jeda tengah-permintaan, namun tetap dimungkinkan lewat serangan MITM aktif.',
    "The encryption provided by TLS may prevent a MITM from reading traffic in-flight, but there's nothing to stop them from delaying the TCP packets on their way from the browser to the web server. By simply delaying the final packet until the web server issues a response, you may be able to desync the browser's connection." => 'Walau TLS mengenkripsi isi, penyerang MITM dapat menahan paket TCP terakhir sebelum sampai ke server hingga timeout terjadi dan koneksi browser terdesinkronisasi.',
    'The flow of this attack is similar to any other client-side desync attack. The user visits a malicious website, which causes their browser to issue a series of cross-domain requests to the target site. In this case, you need to deliberately pad the first request so that the operating system splits it into multiple TCP packets. As you control the padding, you can pad the request until the final packet has a distinct size so you can work out which one to delay.' => 'Penyerang cukup memancing browser mengirim permintaan berukuran tertentu agar terpecah menjadi beberapa paket TCP lalu menunda paket terakhir berukuran khas tersebut.',
    'For an example of how this might look in practice, see [[A0]]Browser-Powered Desync Attacks: A New Frontier in HTTP Request Smuggling[[/A0]].' => 'Contoh praktiknya dibahas lebih lanjut di [[A0]]Browser-Powered Desync Attacks[[/A0]].',
    'How to prevent pause-based desync vulnerabilities' => 'Cara mencegah kerentanan pause-based desync',
    'For some high-level measures you can take to prevent pause-based desync vulnerabilities, and other forms of desync attack, see [[A0]]How to prevent HTTP request smuggling vulnerabilities[[/A0]].' => 'Untuk langkah pencegahan pause-based desync, rujuk [[A0]]Cara mencegah kerentanan HTTP request smuggling[[/A0]].',
];
